window.MODULES = window.MODULES || {};
window.MODULES.CT4107 = {"name":"Entrepreneurship","evidence":"estimated","exam":{"summary":["Format UNKNOWN: no past paper was supplied. The module is assessed largely through a team portfolio (segmentation, persona, QVP, BMC, video pitch), so the exam is most likely applied questions on the same frameworks.","Assumed (not confirmed): 2 hours, answer any 3 of 5 questions, 20 marks each. The three mock papers on this site use that format. Check the real rules with the lecturer (Dr Owen Molloy).","Expect: take a startup idea and (a) segment the market and choose a beachhead, (b) compute a TAM, (c) write an end-user profile / persona / life-cycle use case, (d) write a quantified value proposition and product spec, (e) fill or critique a Business Model Canvas, plus short essays on innovation, creativity and ideation.","Priorities here are ESTIMATED from lecture emphasis (slide counts, worked examples, templates), not from a past-paper tally."],"structure":[{"label":"Assumed paper layout","detail":"5 questions x 20 marks, answer 3 in 120 minutes (about 36 minutes each, 1.8 minutes per mark)."},{"label":"Likely applied question","detail":"A one-paragraph startup scenario, then parts such as: list candidate segments (4-6), score them on the beachhead criteria and justify the pick (8-10 marks); compute TAM bottom-up with stated assumptions (5-6 marks); describe the end-user profile or persona (5-6 marks)."},{"label":"Likely framework question","detail":"Define and apply one framework: Quantified Value Proposition (as-is, to-be, metric), Business Model Canvas (nine blocks filled for a given idea), full life-cycle use case (ten stages), competitive positioning chart."},{"label":"Likely essay question","detail":"Innovation vs invention, types of innovation with examples, creativity (problem finding, relevance, value, novelty), five-step creative process, SCAMPER / SIT, why entrepreneurship is a teachable process (disciplined entrepreneurship)."}],"tips":["Always ground the answer in the given scenario: name the real end user, the real task, real numbers. Generic frameworks without the scenario lose most marks.","State assumptions explicitly for any number (market size, price, share). Show the arithmetic: formula, substitution, result with units (EUR per year).","Use the exact lecture vocabulary: beachhead market, end user vs economic buyer, TAM / SAM / SOM, persona, as-is / to-be state, moat / core, MVBP.","For list-type questions give the name of each item plus one clause of explanation. For \"choose and justify\" questions always show the comparison (a scoring table) and name the decisive factors.","About 1.8 minutes per mark. A 20-mark question: 4 minutes planning, then roughly 1 point-per-2-minutes of writing."],"note":"No past papers exist for this module. Structure, mark values and question types are assumptions built from the lecture deliverables."},"hints":null,"code":"CT4107","topics":[{"id":"beachhead-market","title":"Choosing the beachhead market (Step 2)","priority":1,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high","marks":"10-20 marks (applied)","summary":"**Pick exactly ONE segment to dominate first: score 4-6 candidates on seven criteria (economically attractive, accessible, strong value proposition, complete product, competition, strategic value, personal alignment), drop show-stoppers, rank, and justify the winner.**","mnemonic":"E-A-V-C-C-S-P: Economics, Access, Value, Complete product, Competition, Strategy, Passion","source":["CT4107_Notes_03_Segmentation_Beachhead_TAM","CT4107_Notes_09_Business_Model_Canvas"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definition and why \"beachhead\"","items":["A **beachhead market** is the single, specific market segment you enter first and aim to dominate.","Name comes from D-Day: land on one beach with overwhelming force, then use it as a base to attack neighbouring territory.","Once you hold a dominant share you have the strength, references and cash to attack **adjacent markets** with different offerings, building a larger company with each new following.","A startup has too few resources to serve several segments at once: focus beats breadth."]},{"heading":"Why only ONE market","items":["Focus of scarce resources (money, people, time).","Tailored value proposition, persona and sales process; a single message works.","Word of mouth stays inside one community, so customers refer each other.","Easier to become the market leader (dominant share) and to build a defensible position.","The product can be a **complete (whole) product** for one group, which customers actually buy, rather than half a solution for many."]},{"heading":"Three conditions that define \"a market\" (all must hold)","items":["The customers will **buy the same product** (same needs, same features).","They have a **similar sales process**: same kind of persona, same value proposition, same buying route.","**Word of mouth exists**: they talk to one another, so a satisfied customer sells for you.",{"type":"note","text":"If one of the three fails, you are looking at two markets, not one. Split it further."}]},{"heading":"Process (Step 1 feeds Step 2)","items":["1. Brainstorm segments for the idea (Step 1, market segmentation).","2. Narrow the field to the top **5-6 candidate markets**.","3. Run primary market research on each (interviews, observation, landing pages).","4. Fill in the **Beachhead Market Selection Worksheet**: rate each candidate on each criterion.","5. Eliminate any with a **show stopper**, compute an overall rating, rank 1 (most attractive) to n.","6. Record the **key deciding factors** (what mattered most) and pick the winner.","7. Then define the End-User Profile and persona for it (Steps 3 and 5)."]},{"heading":"The seven criteria (worksheet rows)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Criterion","Question to ask"],"rows":[["1. Economically attractive","Is the segment big enough and rich enough? Can customers pay a price that gives good margins?"],["2. Accessible to our sales force","Can we reach decision makers cheaply (channels, contacts, events, referrals)?"],["3. Strong value proposition","Do they have a compelling, urgent need and will they pay for our benefit? Is it quantifiable?"],["4. Complete product","Can we deliver the full solution (including partners and complementary assets) in a reasonable time?"],["5. Competition","Are we up against entrenched incumbents or is the field open? Competition includes \"do nothing\"."],["6. Strategic value","Does winning this segment open doors to adjacent segments (references, learning, assets, brand)?"],["7. Personal alignment","Does it fit the team: knowledge, contacts, passion, values, location?"]]},"Rating scale per cell: **Very High (best), High, Medium, Low, Show Stopper (worst)**. A show stopper in any one criterion removes the candidate.","Overall rating then ranking: 1 = most attractive; list the **key deciding factors** that drove the ranking."]},{"heading":"Worked scoring: AI form-coach for weightlifting racks","items":["Score 1-5 (5 = Very High). Candidates are the segments brainstormed in the lecture example. Scores are illustrative judgements for an exam answer.",{"type":"table","head":["Criterion","High-end chain gyms","University gyms","Home gyms","Rehab facilities"],"rows":[["Economically attractive","4","3","3","3"],["Accessible to sales force","4","5","2","2"],["Strong value proposition","5","4","3","4"],["Complete product","4","4","5","2"],["Competition","4","4","2","3"],["Strategic value","5","3","3","3"],["Personal alignment","4","5","3","2"],["TOTAL (max 35)","30","28","21","19"],["Rank","1","2","3","4"]]},"Decision: **high-end chain gyms**. Deciding factors: one sale unlocks many sites (accessible), strong need to protect members from injury and justify premium fees (value), and a chain is a lead customer others will copy (strategic value).","University gyms ranked second: easy access through the college but smaller budgets; a good second beachhead later (adjacent market).",{"type":"note","text":"Home gyms have a complete product (easy to ship) but weak access and crowded consumer-fitness competition; rehab facilities need clinical validation, so the product is far from complete."}]},{"heading":"Totals as a chart","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"bar","data":{"title":"Beachhead scores (out of 35)","yLabel":"Total score","bars":[{"label":"High-end chains","value":30},{"label":"University gyms","value":28},{"label":"Home gyms","value":21},{"label":"Rehab","value":19}]},"caption":"Totals from the scoring table above"}]},{"heading":"Lecture extras: examples, funnel and narrowing form","items":["Beachhead strategy (slide wording): focus your resources on one key area, usually a smaller market segment or product category, and win (even dominate) it **before** moving into larger markets.","Proverb on the 'why only one market' slide: a person who chases two rabbits catches neither.","Lecture quiz: **Ryanair's beachhead** = fliers interested in low cost, with the trimmings removed. The slides also ask 'who was their beachhead?' for Facebook, Pinterest and Apple but give no answer (general knowledge, not on slides: Facebook began with college students).","Lecture example result: for the weightlifting smart rack the beachhead chosen was **high-end chain gyms** (examples shown: Equinox, Boston Sports Clubs, LA Fitness, 24 Hour Fitness, Anytime Fitness, David Barton Gym).","The 'Stage 1B: Narrow' form asks six questions per candidate end user, then a rank: (1) is the target customer readily accessible to the sales force? (2) is the target customer well-funded? (3) is there a compelling reason to buy? (4) can you deliver a whole product? (5) is there entrenched competition? (6) can you leverage this segment to enter others? These map onto the seven worksheet criteria.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"row","steps":[{"label":"Brainstorm markets","sub":"Step 1 form"},{"label":"Deeper analysis","sub":"finite set A-E"},{"label":"Pick first-order beachhead","sub":"one segment"},{"label":"Segment it further","sub":"B1-B4"},{"label":"Target customer profile","sub":"Step 3"},{"label":"TAM","sub":"Step 4"},{"label":"Persona","sub":"Step 5"}]},"caption":"Slide: the journey to the holy grail of specificity (narrow until you reach one real representative customer)"}]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Define beachhead and explain the strategy (focus, dominate, then adjacent markets).","Given an idea, list candidate segments, pick one, justify using the criteria.","Why is it risky to target everyone? Why one market?","State the three conditions that define a market.","Discuss a segment that scores high on size but fails on accessibility (the trap: biggest is not best)."]}],"pitfalls":["Picking the biggest market rather than the one you can win first.","Choosing without scoring or without naming deciding factors: the comparison is the marks.","Segments that are too broad (\"students\") so the three market conditions do not hold.","Ignoring \"do nothing\" as a competitor and forgetting complementary assets for the complete product.","Treating a show stopper as just a low score: it eliminates the candidate."]},{"id":"market-segmentation","title":"Market segmentation and the segmentation matrix (Step 1)","priority":2,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high","marks":"8-15 marks (applied)","summary":"**Step 1: brainstorm many possible market segments for the technology/idea, describe each in a segmentation matrix (end user, task, benefit, urgency, size, competition...), and narrow to 5-6 candidates.**","mnemonic":"WHO does WHAT, why NOW, how MANY, vs WHOM","source":["CT4107_Notes_03_Segmentation_Beachhead_TAM","CT4107_Notes_09_Business_Model_Canvas"],"sections":[{"heading":"What it is","items":["Market segmentation = dividing a customer base into groups of people who are similar in specific ways (age, gender, interests, spending habits, task, need).","**Step 1 of Aulet's 24 steps**: market evaluation. Start from the idea/technology and ask \"who could use this and for what?\".","Brainstorm widely (industries, user types, applications, geography), then **narrow the field to the top 5-6 candidate markets** using primary market research.","Think through the **end-to-end customer journey as the customer sees it**, not just the touchpoints you control (McKinsey)."]},{"heading":"Segmentation matrix: fields","items":["Name the segment precisely: capture exactly the group you want and no more (can be general at first, narrow later).",{"type":"table","head":["Field","What to write"],"rows":[["End user","The real person who uses the product (not the economic buyer, not an organisation)"],["Task","What the end user does that you significantly affect or let them do for the first time"],["Benefit","What the end user gains"],["Urgency of need","How urgent is the problem or opportunity?"],["Example end users","Named people you have or will talk to, to validate"],["Lead customers","Influential (lighthouse) customers whom others will follow"],["Willingness to change","How conservative is the segment? Is there a crisis forcing change?"],["Frequency of buying","Buying cycle: how often do they buy new products?"],["Concentration of buyers","Monopoly, oligopoly (few buyers) or many competitive buyers?"],["Other market considerations","High staff turnover, thin margins, high growth, word-of-mouth virality..."],["Size of market","Number of end users, to an order of magnitude (10s, 100s, 1Ks, 10Ks, 100Ks, 1M)"],["Est. value of end user","First-pass annual value per end user ($1, $10, $100, $1K...)"],["Competition / alternatives","From the end user's view, including the \"do nothing\" option"],["Other components for a full solution","Complementary assets you do not have but need (the whole product)"],["Important partners","Distributors / vendors you must fit into (workflow or channel)"],["Other personal considerations","Geography, fit with team values, existing knowledge and contacts"]]}]},{"heading":"Worked example: weightlifting-form technology","items":["Idea: machine learning and computer vision to check lifting technique and give instant feedback (a smart rack).","Candidate segments brainstormed: ordinary gyms, high-end gyms, university gyms, home gyms, rehab facilities, apartments/hotels, Olympic athletes.","Each gets a column in the matrix, then primary research cuts the list before the beachhead scoring.","The other example (Urban Cake Shop) shows segmenting by **behaviour**: morning commuters, seasonal celebrators, one-time weddings, casual drop-ins, each with different motivation and price sensitivity."]},{"heading":"Segmentation bases to mention","items":["**Demographic**: age, gender, income, job title, education, family status.","**Geographic**: country, urban/rural, region.","**Psychographic**: values, aspirations, fears, lifestyle.","**Behavioural**: buying frequency, usage, loyalty, occasion, benefit sought.","In entrepreneurship the best segments are defined by **end user + task + need**, not demographics alone."]},{"heading":"Step 1 forms and lecture examples","items":["Step 1 is called **market evaluation**. **Stage 1 Brainstorm form**: 'What is my idea or technology? What industries and end users could my idea or technology apply to?' with three columns: industry or similar category, end users, what they would use it for.","**Stage 1B Narrow form** columns: end user | target customer readily accessible to sales force? | well-funded? | compelling reason to buy? | can you deliver a whole product? | entrenched competition? | can you leverage this segment to enter others? | rank. Output: the top 5-6 candidates.","Slide 'Very important step': see the world through the eyes of the **customer** versus through the perspective of the **company**. Segment from the customer's view.","Week 4 deliverable = market segmentation, beachhead market analysis and end-user profiling.",{"type":"note","text":"McKinsey's six actions for managing customer-experience journeys (slides): (1) step back and identify the journeys customers take, from the customer's point of view; (2) understand how they navigate across touchpoints; (3) anticipate needs, expectations and desires at each part of the journey; (4) understand what is working and what is not; (5) set priorities for the most important gaps and opportunities; (6) fix root-cause issues and redesign the journeys end to end."},"**Pregnancy-tracking app** example: market segments = pregnant women, first-time mothers, fathers-to-be, family, close friends, doctors. The lecture picks **first-time mothers** (and asks what the main requirements of such an app are).","**Weightlifting rack (Perch-style)** segmentation matrix, columns: chain gyms (discount/mainstream), chain gyms (high end), varsity athletic weight rooms (college), professional weight rooms, home gyms, rehab facilities, apartments/offices, Olympic training facilities, high-end non-chain gyms. Rows: examples, pros, cons, motivations of the economic buyer, regulatory climate, competitive environment, early adopters.","High-end chain gyms, **pros**: less concerned about equipment price; more concerned with image; easier to expand to other locations and to follow-on markets; large market size. **Cons**: current lack of contacts; equipment must be extremely good quality; improving athletes is not their main motivation (value proposition mismatch). Other potential markets: varsity/professional athletics programmes, home gyms, mainstream/budget gyms."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Segment the market for idea X: list at least 4-6 distinct segments with a differentiator for each.","Explain the difference between end user, economic buyer and customer (Step 12 introduces the buyer/champion).","Fill a segmentation matrix row for one segment.","Explain why segmentation must be backed by primary market research, not desk guesses."]}],"pitfalls":["Listing industries instead of end users with a task (\"healthcare\" is not a segment).","Writing an organisation as the end user; it must be a real person.","Forgetting urgency and willingness to change: a big segment that does not care is no market.","No evidence from interviews/observation."]},{"id":"tam-sam-som","title":"TAM, SAM, SOM and calculating TAM (Step 4)","priority":3,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high","marks":"5-10 marks (calculation)","summary":"**TAM = annual revenue you would earn at 100% share of the beachhead market; estimate bottom-up (end users x units x purchases per year x price), cross-check top-down; SAM is the part you can serve, SOM the part you can realistically win.**","mnemonic":"TAM = Total, SAM = Serve, SOM = Snatch (obtain)","source":["CT4107_Notes_03_Segmentation_Beachhead_TAM"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions","items":["**TAM (Total Addressable Market)**: total market demand for the product, with no limits on geography, price or distribution. Aulet's Step 4: the **annual revenue (per year) you would earn if you won 100% share** of the beachhead market.","**SAM (Serviceable Available Market)**: the part of TAM you can realistically target and serve, given geography, pricing, regulation and distribution.","**SOM (Serviceable Obtainable Market)**: the part of SAM you can realistically capture given sales/marketing capability, competition and market saturation.","Always TAM >= SAM >= SOM. A funnel: TAM (everyone) then SAM (those we can serve) then SOM (those we will win).","Purpose: is the beachhead **big enough to be interesting** (to you, to investors), and is it sensible to start there?"]},{"heading":"Top-down vs bottom-up","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","Top-down","Bottom-up"],"rows":[["Method","Start from a big industry figure (population, total market from reports) and cut it down by percentages","Build from the unit: number of end users x units x purchase frequency x price"],["Data","Industry research, census, analyst reports","Own research, interviews, price tests, comparables"],["Strength","Quick; good sanity check","Credible, defensible, ties to your price and customers"],["Weakness","Easy to overstate (\"1% of a huge market\"); not tied to real buyers","Needs realistic data and estimates; more work"],["Lecture view","Use as a cross-check","The effective approach: needs **realistic data and realistic estimation**"]]}]},{"heading":"Bottom-up formula (lecture)","items":[{"type":"formula","label":"Bottom-up TAM (per year)","text":"TAM = N x Q x F x P"},"N = total population of potential end users in the beachhead.","Q = how many units each buys at a time.","F = purchase frequency (purchases per year).","P = intended selling price per unit.","Aulet's workbook estimates the **annualised revenue per end user** three ways and takes a consensus range: (I) one-time charge: (price x units) / average life in years; (II) budget: end user's budget x % that could reasonably go to this solution; (III) comparables converted to annual revenue. Then TAM = number of end users x annual revenue per user.",{"type":"formula","label":"One-time charge, annualised","text":"Annual revenue per user = (price per unit x units per user) / average life of product in years"},{"type":"formula","label":"Budget method","text":"Annual revenue per user = total budget per user x % of budget that could go to this solution"}]},{"heading":"Worked example 1: top-down then bottom-up (pregnancy app, Ireland)","items":["Top-down funnel from the lecture (countrymeters): females in Ireland 2,465,802, then pregnancies per year about 75,713, then first-time mothers about 30,306 (40% of pregnancies).","Bottom-up for an app: N = 30,306 first-time mothers, Q = 1 app per mother, F = 1 purchase (one-time, per pregnancy), price assumed EUR 5.",{"type":"formula","label":"Result","text":"TAM = 30,306 x 1 x 1 x EUR 5 = EUR 151,530 per year"},"Reading: a very small market (about EUR 150k a year) unless a higher price, a subscription or the wider EU is added. This answers \"is it big enough to be interesting?\" with: probably not as a standalone beachhead.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"bar","data":{"title":"Top-down funnel, Ireland (people)","yLabel":"People","bars":[{"label":"Females","value":2465802},{"label":"Pregnant per year","value":75713},{"label":"First-time mothers","value":30306}]},"caption":"Each filter cuts the population down to the real end users"}]},{"heading":"Worked example 2: Perch-style smart gym racks (USA)","items":["From the lecture: 35,000 gyms in the USA, of which 3,000 are high-end chain gyms; each gym would buy 4 racks.","Units in beachhead = 3,000 x 4 = **12,000 units** (the lecture's \"12k+ units\"). Selling to all 35,000 gyms would be 140,000 units.","Assume (for the exam) a price of USD 5,000 per rack: TAM = 12,000 x 5,000 = **USD 60,000,000** one-time.","If a rack lasts 5 years and you annualise: 60,000,000 / 5 = **USD 12,000,000 per year**.","Note how the beachhead (3,000 chain gyms) gives a much smaller but more focused TAM than all gyms.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"bar","data":{"title":"Rack units (USA)","yLabel":"Units","bars":[{"label":"All 35,000 gyms x4","value":140000},{"label":"3,000 high-end x4","value":12000}]},"caption":"TAM of the beachhead vs the whole gym market"}]},{"heading":"Worked example 3: per-user annual revenue (workbook method)","items":["(I) One-time: price 1,200, 1 unit, life 4 years: 1,200 x 1 / 4 = **300 per year**.","(II) Budget: budget 10,000, 5% could go to this solution: 10,000 x 0.05 = **500 per year**.","(III) Comparable product: 400 per year.","Consensus range 300-500; take the mid-point **400** as the working figure. With 3,000 end users: TAM = 3,000 x 400 = **1,200,000 per year**."]},{"heading":"More lecture material: sanity check, three levels, rental example, top-down worksheet","items":["Step 4 slide: the beachhead TAM calculation is your **sanity check that you are headed in the right direction**. Sweet spot = big enough to get positive cash flow; too small = uninteresting; too big = danger (too big to veer, you cannot dominate it).","The lecture asks 'is the market big enough to be interesting?' and 'what % of the global market can we capture, how many customers can we obtain and what is the revenue per customer?'.","Make a **first-pass TAM for your first (beachhead) market only**. The strong preference is **bottom-up**: show from primary market research how many of the potential customers you identified and extrapolate to the broader market. Top-down (market-analysis reports, extrapolating without direct interaction and validation) is complementary but much less compelling, because very important subtleties are missed.","Venn on the Step 4 slides: **Total** market (everyone), **Served available** market (how many can I reach with my sales channel), **Target** market (for a startup: who will be the initial buyers). Note: slides label TAM both 'Total Addressable' and 'Total Available' market; use the Step 4 definition (annual revenue at 100% share of the beachhead).",{"type":"table","head":["Level (US rental units example)","Units","Revenue","How it is built"],"rows":[["Total available market: all US rental units","42.58M","USD 1.02B per year","42.58M x 2 residents per unit x USD 1 per month x 12"],["Served available market: Manhattan rental units","0.59M","USD 14.2M per year","0.59M x 2 x USD 1 x 12 = 14.16M"],["Target market: Manhattan high-rise buildings with over 100 units","190K","USD 1.4M per year","190K x 2 x 30% adoption x USD 1 x 12 = 1.368M"]]},"Sources cited on the slide: US Dept of Housing and Urban Development 2015; National Multifamily Housing Council; landlordstation.com. Assumptions stated on the slide: 2 residents per unit; 30% user adoption; USD 1 per month per user (always state assumptions and sources).","Top-down estimate **worksheet** (funnel): number of people in your largest demographic or psychographic characteristic, then 1st segmentation based on end-user profile (% of previous segment, assumptions, sources), then 2nd and 3rd segmentation, ending with the **end users in the beachhead market**. Every filter needs a stated %, an assumption and a source.","Smart-rack funnel from the slides (Perch example): 35,000 gyms in the USA, then 3,000 high-end chain gyms, then 4 racks each, giving TAM of 12k+ units. Total market context: 34 million Americans weightlifted in 2014, up 38% since 2000. Follow-on markets: mainstream chain/franchised gyms, budget gyms. Other potential beachheads: sports performance facilities, professional/college facilities.","Perch (the slide's example product) is software-enabled gym hardware that uses computer vision and machine learning to track movement during weightlifting and give instant feedback; slide claims: versatile, compact (attaches to any rack in minutes), passive (no wearables).","Workbook layout for annualised revenue per end user: I one-time charge (price per unit, units per end user, average life, annualised = price x units / life), II budget available (current spend, total budget, % that could go to this solution, annualised = budget x %), III comparables (who and which products, converted to annualised revenue), IV interpretation (consensus range, and how you reached it)."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Define TAM, SAM, SOM with an example for a given idea.","Compute a bottom-up TAM from given data and state assumptions.","Compare top-down and bottom-up; which would an investor trust?","Is the market big enough? Interpret the number (very small = too niche; very large and diffuse = not a beachhead)."]}],"pitfalls":["Using the \"1% of a huge market\" shortcut: it is unconvincing and not tied to buyers.","Forgetting the time unit: TAM in the lecture is **per year**; if a product lasts several years, annualise it.","Mixing end users with economic buyers (count the people who actually pay).","Calculating TAM for the whole idea rather than for the **beachhead** only.","No stated assumptions: every figure needs a source or a labelled assumption."]},{"id":"qvp","title":"Quantified Value Proposition (Steps 8-9)","priority":4,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high","marks":"6-12 marks","summary":"**QVP = convert the benefit for the persona into one tangible metric on the persona's #1 priority: As-Is state (X) versus Possible/To-Be state (Y), so value = difference of X and Y.**","mnemonic":"QVP: X (as-is) to Y (to-be) on Priority #1, in numbers","source":["CT4107_Notes_07_Product_Spec_QVP"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definition","items":["A **Quantified Value Proposition** converts the benefits your persona gets into a tangible **metric aligned with the persona's top priority** (sometimes top priorities).","Core question: \"what value am I getting from this product?\" answered in numbers, because **if you cannot measure it you cannot demonstrate added value**.","Focus on what the customer **gains**, not on technology, features and functions.","Build it, illustrate it and **validate it with multiple target customers**.","Do not forget the other priorities: ensure nothing decreases in the persona's other key priorities that would offset your benefit."]},{"heading":"How to build it (steps)","items":["1. Take the persona's **#1 priority** (the thing they care most about); this is the dimension on which value is measured.","2. State the **As-Is state**: what do customers achieve on that dimension today (current solution, or do nothing)?","3. State the **Possible (To-Be) state**: what could be achieved with your product?","4. The value proposition is the **difference** between X (as-is) and Y (to-be), expressed in the same unit.","5. Make sure it is a **significant improvement** and easy to buy; back the claim with the support that underpins it (pilot data, research, test results).","6. Validate with customers; iterate."]},{"heading":"Template","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Part","Fill in"],"rows":[["Persona and priority #1","e.g. gym owner, member retention"],["As-Is state (X)","measured today on the priority metric"],["To-Be state (Y)","measured with the product"],["Quantified value","Y - X (and % change, time/money saved)"],["Support (why believable)","pilot, benchmark, research reference"],["Check other priorities","confirm no decrease elsewhere"]]},"Sentence form: \"For [persona], whose top priority is [P], our product moves [metric] from [X] to [Y], saving/earning [Z] per [period].\""]},{"heading":"Pains and gains (from benefits to metric)","items":["List the persona's **pains** (what goes wrong, obstacles) and **gains** (what they want and how they measure success).","Student accommodation app example: pains = search takes too long and is stressful, landlords respond slowly, no unified search app; gains = find clean suitable places near college, less stress. A quantified version: \"reduce time to find accommodation from 6 weeks to 1 week\"."]},{"heading":"Lecture examples (worked)","items":["**Bovine disease detection (Meater, \"faster quarantine of sick animals\")**: BRD symptoms appear biologically 2-4 days before cattle look visibly ill. Slide tree for 200 cattle that develop BRD: **As-Is** 50 deaths plus 150 illnesses = loss to disease **USD 63k**; **with Meater** 25 deaths plus 175 illnesses = loss **USD 40k**; **saving USD 23k**. Sale price USD 7 per cow and the device can be reused four times.","Check from the slide unit costs: death = USD 997 lost revenue per head; each illness = treatment 16 + reduced feed efficiency 15 + lower-quality grade 58 = USD 89 per head. As-Is: 50 x 997 + 150 x 89 = 49,850 + 13,350 = 63,200. With sensor: 25 x 997 + 175 x 89 = 24,925 + 15,575 = 40,500. Saving = 22,700, about 23k.","Check the saving: 63,000 - 40,000 = **23,000** per year.","**X-treme sunscreen for athletes**: lasts 6 times longer, 5 times more effective, 3 times less quantity applied; stays 100% effective after all events. Every claim is a multiplier on the athlete's priority (protection during events).","Another: a collaboration tool for ideation and design that reduces production time and accelerates product launches (metric: weeks to launch)."]},{"heading":"QVP vs value proposition vs USP","items":["**Value proposition**: the promise of benefit to a customer segment (qualitative).","**QVP**: the same promise **with numbers** on the persona's #1 priority.","**USP (unique selling proposition)**: what makes you different from alternatives; sustained over time by your **core** (the moat).","In the lecture exercise the three are separate questions: \"Value proposition? QVP? USP?\""]},{"heading":"Chart: as-is vs to-be (bovine example)","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"bar","data":{"title":"Loss to disease (USD thousand)","yLabel":"USD k","bars":[{"label":"As-Is","value":63},{"label":"With sensor","value":40},{"label":"Saving","value":23}]},"caption":"QVP: loss falls from 63k to 40k, a saving of 23k"}]},{"heading":"Value proposition: four essential elements (slide)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Element","Question","Content"],"rows":[["1 The customer's perspective","Customer needs and insights","What problems do they need to solve? How can they increase sales? How can they reduce costs?"],["2 What's in it for them?","Promise of value that resonates","Benefits that add value; solving their urgent problems; why they should care about your solution"],["3 Why choose to buy from you?","Competitive differentiation","Demonstrate points of difference that matter to customers; put action plans in place to improve points of parity"],["4 Prove it","Proof and quantification","Create trust (why should the customer believe you?); quantify the benefits in your customers' language"]]},"A value proposition shows how products or services create value for a **specific customer segment**. Element 4 is what the QVP adds.","Slide on measurement (Lord Kelvin): if you cannot express what you are talking about in numbers, your knowledge is meagre; hence 'what value do I get out of this product?' must become a tangible metric (benefit to metric).","**Gain creators and pain relievers** (value-proposition-canvas language): list the persona's pains (up to 5) and gains (up to 5); your products and services act as pain relievers and gain creators; link back to the persona and user profile.","The chain of steps: user profile, then persona with ranked priorities, then pains and gains, then QVP (as-is X, possible state Y, value = difference measured on priority #1), which also feeds competitive positioning. 'You have to be sure there is no decrease in the other key priorities that offsets the benefits you create.'"]},{"heading":"More lecture examples with numbers","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Example: ideation-to-launch collaboration tool","Ideation","Design","Production","Commercialisation"],"rows":[["As-Is","3 weeks","10 days","1 week","1 month"],["To-Be","2 weeks","5 days","1 week","1 month"],["Reduction","33%","50%","0%","0%"]]},"Overall: 21+10+7+30 = 68 days as-is versus 14+5+7+30 = 56 days to-be, an **18% reduction** in time to launch (12/68 = 17.6%). Slide rule: **have references or justification for every figure**.",{"type":"table","head":["Sunscreen for athletes","Existing sunscreen","X-treme sunscreen"],"rows":[["Triathletes (events of about 2-10 hours: swim, bike, run)","Gone after the swim","Effective after all events"],["Marathon runners (about 4 hours)","Effectiveness falls from 100% to 45% to 20% during the run","100% effective after all events"],["Claims","-","Lasts 6 times longer; 5 times more effective; 3 times less quantity applied"]]},"**Sports application** (help people engage in new sports): pains = students find it difficult to join sports clubs; clubs find it difficult to recruit members. VP = students remain active and become more social; clubs regain members, increase revenue and boost morale. Note the VP is stated qualitatively; a QVP would add numbers (e.g. membership sign-ups).","Exercise on slides: for **Ryanair**, state the value proposition, the QVP and the USP (three different things)."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Write a QVP for a given product and persona (with assumed numbers, labelled as hypotheses).","Explain why quantifying matters and why benefits beat features.","Critique a weak QVP (vague \"better\", no baseline, wrong priority).","Link QVP to competitive positioning (the same two priorities become the chart axes)."]}],"pitfalls":["Describing features (\"uses machine learning\") instead of the benefit in the customer's metric.","No As-Is baseline: an improvement from nothing is meaningless.","Measuring on the wrong priority (not the persona's #1).","Inventing precise numbers with no support; present them as pilot targets to validate.","Ignoring side effects on other priorities (cost, usability) that cancel the gain."]},{"id":"business-model-canvas","title":"Business Model Canvas: the nine blocks","priority":5,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high","marks":"10-20 marks","summary":"**The BMC (Strategyzer/Osterwalder) is a one-page template with nine blocks (customer segments, value propositions, channels, customer relationships, revenue streams, key resources, key activities, key partnerships, cost structure) showing how a business creates, delivers and captures value.**","mnemonic":"WHO (segments, relationships, channels), WHAT (value), HOW (activities, resources, partners), MONEY (revenue, costs)","source":["CT4107_Notes_09_Business_Model_Canvas","CT4107_Notes_09b_What_Is_a_Business_Model_Canvas"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definition","items":["The **Business Model Canvas (BMC)** is a strategic management template for developing new or documenting existing business models: a visual chart with elements describing the firm's or product's **value proposition, infrastructure, customers and finances**.","Nine building blocks cover four areas: **customers, product/service, infrastructure/operations, financial viability**.","It shows how the business intends to make money; works equally for social enterprises (customers = clients, beneficiaries or audiences).","Advantage over a full plan: you can start getting **customer feedback sooner** and adjust as the model evolves."]},{"heading":"Canvas layout","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Business Model Canvas layout","directed":false,"r":20,"height":420,"nodes":[{"id":"kp","label":"Key partnerships","sub":"8","x":10,"y":38,"color":2},{"id":"ka","label":"Key activities","sub":"7","x":31,"y":22,"color":2},{"id":"kr","label":"Key resources","sub":"6","x":31,"y":56,"color":2},{"id":"vp","label":"Value propositions","sub":"2","x":52,"y":38,"color":0},{"id":"cr","label":"Customer relationships","sub":"4","x":73,"y":22,"color":1},{"id":"ch","label":"Channels","sub":"3","x":73,"y":56,"color":1},{"id":"cs","label":"Customer segments","sub":"1","x":92,"y":38,"color":1},{"id":"co","label":"Cost structure","sub":"9","x":25,"y":90,"color":3},{"id":"re","label":"Revenue streams","sub":"5","x":75,"y":90,"color":3}],"edges":[{"from":"vp","to":"cs"},{"from":"ka","to":"vp"},{"from":"kr","to":"vp"},{"from":"kp","to":"ka"},{"from":"cs","to":"re"},{"from":"ka","to":"co"}]},"caption":"Right side = customers (who), middle = value (what), left = infrastructure (how), bottom = finance"}]},{"heading":"The nine blocks: question and what to write","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Block","Key question","Contents / prompts"],"rows":[["1 Customer segments","Who do you sell to and help?","Groups you create value for; characteristics and personas; segmentation by age, gender, interests, spending habits; mass vs niche vs multi-sided"],["2 Value propositions","What are your promises?","Problems solved, needs met, value delivered per segment; what is unique and why they prefer you to alternatives"],["3 Channels","How do you reach them?","Avenues by which customers contact you and enter the sales cycle: communicate, sell, deliver, service; manage the journey from awareness to sale"],["4 Customer relationships","How do you interact?","Face-to-face or remote; acquire, retain, upsell; added value"],["5 Revenue streams","How much income do customers generate?","How the value proposition converts to financial gain: sources, pricing strategy, mix by segment, ancillary services"],["6 Key resources","What resources are required?","Physical, intellectual property, human, financial (credit, working capital)"],["7 Key activities","What tasks are key?","Actions needed to deliver value: R&D, production, marketing, sales, service, finance, admin; processes for efficiency"],["8 Key partnerships","Which external parties are critical?","Suppliers, collaborators, distributors; what they do and provide; how to manage them"],["9 Cost structure","What does it cost to launch and run?","Costs per stage (marketing, acquiring customers, team, partners, distribution); biggest cost driver; fixed vs variable; economies of scale"]]}]},{"heading":"Worked example: Groupon (lecture)","items":["**Customer segments**: two-sided: consumers (local, affluent, students, singles) and merchants (local shops, SMEs, global brands, event organisers).","**Value propositions**: consumers get discounts/bargains (crowd-sourced buying power); merchants get effective advertising, a fast-built prospect list, a sell-and-fulfilment platform.","**Channels**: email campaigns, website, social networks, mobile apps, B2B sales force, merchant web portal.","**Customer relationships**: price/convenience, trust, virality for consumers; consulting on offer preparation for merchants.","**Revenue streams**: percentage fee on transactions (ancillary: new products such as Gnow and Glive, credit cards).","**Key resources**: databases, creative/publisher team, sales force and merchant database, web platform and software developers.","**Key activities**: editorial/content and email marketing scheduling, B2B sales with city planner, contract management, business intelligence and profiling, M&A.","**Key partners**: internet and coupon providers, POS and credit systems.","**Cost structure**: consumer acquisition (marketing), merchant acquisition, IT and data-centre connectivity, editorial content."]},{"heading":"Worked example: filling the BMC for a startup (smart rack for gym chains)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Block","Entry"],"rows":[["Customer segments","High-end chain gyms (buyer: operations director; end user: lifter aged 25-45)"],["Value proposition","Fewer injuries and better retention: instant form feedback; target 30% fewer injury incidents (to validate)"],["Channels","Trade shows, direct sales to chain head offices, installer network"],["Customer relationships","Dedicated account manager, pilot-then-roll-out, monthly performance report"],["Revenue streams","Hardware lease plus monthly software subscription per rack"],["Key resources","Computer-vision models and training data (IP), engineers, camera hardware supply"],["Key activities","Model development, hardware integration, installation and support, sales"],["Key partnerships","Rack manufacturer, camera supplier, gym chain pilot partners, installers"],["Cost structure","Engineering salaries (largest), hardware cost of goods, sales, cloud hosting; mostly fixed early, variable with units"]]}]},{"heading":"How to complete the canvas well","items":["Start with **customer segments and value propositions**, then channels/relationships, then revenue, then resources/activities/partners, then costs.","Be consistent: each value proposition needs a segment, a channel and a revenue stream; every cost should link to a key activity or resource.","Treat entries as hypotheses and test them; a canvas is a living document.","Keep it to one page; clearest possible picture of the existing or proposed model."]},{"heading":"Lecture block definitions and guiding questions","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Block","Lecture definition","Strategyzer guiding question (slides)"],"rows":[["Customer segments","Practice of dividing a customer base into groups similar in specific ways: age, gender, interests, spending habits","Which customers and users are you serving? Which jobs do they really want to get done?"],["Value proposition","The fundamental concept of the exchange of value between your business and your customer/clients","What are you offering them? What is that getting done for them? Do they care?"],["Channels","The avenues through which your customer comes into contact with your business and becomes part of your sales cycle","How does each customer segment want to be reached? Through which interaction points?"],["Customer relationships","How a business interacts with its customers: **get, keep, grow**","What relationships are you establishing with each segment: personal, automated, acquisitive, retentive?"],["Revenue streams","The way by which your business converts your value proposition or solution to the customer's problem into financial gain","What are customers really willing to pay for? How? Transactional or recurring revenue?"],["Key resources","The resources your business requires to do business","Which resources underpin your business model? Which assets are essential? (financial, physical, IP, HR)"],["Key activities","The actions your business undertakes to achieve the value proposition for your customers","Which activities do you need to perform well? What is crucial? (manufacturing, software, supply chain)"],["Key partners","A list of other external companies/suppliers/parties you may need to achieve your key activities and deliver value to the customer","Which partners and suppliers leverage your model? Who do you need to rely on? What do we get from and give them?"],["Cost structure","The monetary cost of operating as a business","What is the resulting cost structure? Which key elements drive costs? Fixed or variable?"]]},{"type":"note","text":"Two numbering orders appear. The Santander guide lists: 1 customer segments, 2 value propositions, 3 channels, 4 customer relationships, 5 revenue streams, 6 key resources, 7 key activities, 8 key partnerships, 9 cost. The lecture's yellow-template slides number the fill-in order as 1 customer segments, 2 value propositions, 3 channels, 4 customer relationships, 5 key activities, 6 key resources, 7 key partners, 8 revenue streams, 9 cost structure ('What is the most NB?'). Numbering is only an order of working; learn all nine blocks and the question each answers."},"Alternative labels on one canvas slide: partner network (key partners), offer (value proposition), distribution channels (channels). Cost structure and revenue streams sit along the bottom."]},{"heading":"More lecture examples: Ordr, LinkedIn and the bottom line","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Ordr (student project: platform that makes queueing for payment in pubs easy)","Canvas entries"],"rows":[["Customer segments","Bar managers, publicans, pub goers, drinks companies, social events, advertisers, till companies, drinks promoters"],["Value propositions","Speeds up the ordering process; more convenient payment for customers and the bar; guarantees customers are served in the correct order; avoids long queues"],["Channels","Virtual; mobile application stores; selling directly to pubs"],["Customer relationships","Social media; special offers; loyalty programme"],["Key activities","Installation of the product in the bar; sales; development and innovation of the mobile application"],["Key resources","Strong team of developers; in-depth knowledge of the bar trade; experienced sales team"],["Key partners","Event organisers, individual super-pubs, drinks companies (strategic alliances); a local IT admin company for installation; an online company paid to refer traffic to the website"],["Cost structure","Salaries, IT equipment, office, installation, marketing, maintenance"],["Revenue streams","Percentage of each transaction through the app; annual subscription fee; free installation and free app; advertising"]]},"**LinkedIn** canvas (businessmodelgeneration.com): segments = internet users, recruiters, advertisers and marketers, developers; value = manage professional identity and build a network, identify and reach the right talent, reach the target audience, access to the LinkedIn database via APIs and widgets; relationships = same-side and cross-side network effects; channels = website, mobile apps, field sales; revenue = free offerings and premium subscriptions, hiring solutions, marketing solutions; key activity = platform development; key resource = the LinkedIn platform; partners = Equinix (data centre), content providers; costs = web hosting, marketing and sales, product development, general and administrative.","**Bottom line** slide: no revenue model means no business; if you can cure a pain you can generate revenue; if there is no pain the best revenue model will not help; until someone pays you, it is just an idea. 'Revenue model = revenue streams such as ads and subscriptions.'"]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Name and explain the nine building blocks.","Complete a BMC for a given startup (table with all nine blocks).","Differences between business model and business plan; between BMC and a plan.","Identify revenue streams and cost drivers; discuss a two-sided model (Groupon).","Critique a BMC for inconsistency (e.g. free product but no revenue stream)."]}],"pitfalls":["Missing blocks or mixing them (channels vs customer relationships; key resources vs key activities).","Generic statements not tied to the scenario.","Revenue stream stated without how it is priced (per unit? subscription? fee on transaction?).","Forgetting that multi-sided businesses need a canvas entry per side.","Calling the canvas a business plan: the plan is far more detailed, with strategy and financial forecasts over years."]},{"id":"customer-persona","title":"Customer / buyer persona (Step 5)","priority":6,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high","marks":"6-10 marks","summary":"**A persona is a fictional but realistic, named individual in the beachhead market who best exemplifies the End-User Profile: demographics, psychographics, goals, pains, behaviours, with a picture, used as the touchstone for every decision.**","mnemonic":"PERSONA = Person with Pains, Goals, Gains: one face for the whole market","source":["CT4107_Notes_04_Customer_Persona","CT4107_Notes_03_Segmentation_Beachhead_TAM"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definition","items":["A **customer/buyer persona** is a fictional portrait of a \"real\" person who buys from (or uses) your brand, based on analysis of demographics, behaviours and motivations.","It is **one end user, from one potential customer, who best exemplifies your End-User Profile**; make the customer tangible, unambiguous and concrete.","It describes how the persona will interact with the product and how we reach and sell to them.","Purpose: identify basic info, personality, pictures, pain points and goals; create **focus** in the organisation; be a **touchstone for all decisions** (\"when a key decision is made, who is making it and what are their needs?\").","Use it to tailor content, messaging, features and sales approach."]},{"heading":"Profile vs persona","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","Profile","Persona"],"rows":[["Describes","Average customer group (a type of buyer)","One specific representative (fictional) person"],["Level","Statistical, general","Concrete: name, photo, story"],["Used for","Sizing (TAM), targeting, segmentation","Design, messaging, decisions, QVP and use case"],["Risk","\"Average customer\" does not exist","Stereotyping if not based on research"]]}]},{"heading":"Demographics vs psychographics","items":["**Demographics**: grouping by external factors such as age, gender, race, income; statistical and quantitative.","**Psychographics**: qualitative study of psychological characteristics: values, desires, goals, interests, lifestyle choices."]},{"heading":"Persona template (fields)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Field","Content"],"rows":[["Name and picture","A name and a photo make the person memorable"],["Background","Job, education, family, location, story"],["Age and gender","Demographic basics"],["Behaviours and spending habits","How they buy, what they use, where they spend"],["Personality","Traits, attitudes to technology, risk"],["Goals, needs, motivations","What she wants and aspires to; how she measures success"],["Pain points","Frustrations and obstacles; what a bad day looks like"],["Priorities","Ranked and weighted (link to QVP)"],["How to reach her","Watering holes, channels, influences"]]}]},{"heading":"Pains and gains prompts","items":["**Pains**: What does a bad day look like for her? What is she afraid of? What keeps her awake at night? What is she responsible for? What obstacles stand in her way?","**Gains**: What does she want and aspire to? How does she measure success? How could she benefit? What can we offer her?"]},{"heading":"Example (cycling app, London)","items":["From user interviews three user groups were found and turned into personas: the **Regular Cyclist** (cycles to work daily, owns a bike, believes cycling saves time and money), the **Environmentalist** (cuts carbon footprint), and the main persona **Charlotte, the Scared Cyclist**.","Lesson: you may identify several groups, but choose **one main persona** (from the beachhead) to design for."]},{"heading":"Persona templates seen in the lecture","items":["Slide checklist of persona fields: pictures, background, age, gender, behaviours, personality, goals, needs, motivations, pain points, spending habits.","Slide: 'Design a product which has a purpose for the person using it.' Persona = a specific (representative but fictional) buyer **and how they will interact with the product and how we reach or sell to them**.","**Semrush-style persona cards**: name and photo, bio, a quote, pain points (frustrations), gains/expectations from my product, **factors influencing buying decisions**, communication channels (channels, content types and formats, topics), demographic info (age, location, family status, education, income), **jobs to be done**, industry experience and knowledge. Examples: a working mother buying a house (age 37, married with 2 children, graduate degree, income USD 150k+) and a small-agency founder (age 36, San Francisco).","**Xtensio-style persona (Charlotte)**: goals (save money, look presentable, not die), frustrations (underground smells, scared of cyclists shouting, does not know how to dock a bike), bio, quote ('I want everything to be quick and easy'), traits (practical, organised, clean), personality sliders (introvert/extrovert, thinking/feeling, sensing/intuition, judging/perceiving), brands and influencers, technology use (internet, mobile, social media, software). Facts: age 23, law student, single, London, character 'Logistician' (ISTJ).","Persona and life-cycle use case are the week 6 deliverable; the previous step (profile) is the week 4 deliverable."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Define a persona and explain why it is used.","Write a persona for a given idea (name, demographics, psychographics, goals, pains, behaviours).","Distinguish profile and persona; demographic and psychographic.","How is a persona built? (interviews, observation, surveys, analytics.)"]}],"pitfalls":["A persona that is an average of everyone, or several people merged.","Pure demographics with no motivations or pains.","Invented without research evidence.","Picking a persona outside the beachhead segment.","Confusing the end user with the economic buyer (the buyer persona is a separate step later)."]},{"id":"life-cycle-use-case","title":"Full life-cycle use case / customer journey (Step 6)","priority":7,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high","marks":"6-12 marks","summary":"**The Full Life-Cycle Use Case describes ten stages of how your persona determines the need, finds, analyses, acquires, pays for, installs, uses, values, rebuys and recommends the product, with who/when/where/how at each stage.**","mnemonic":"NEED-FIND-WEIGH-GET-PAY-SET UP-USE-VALUE-REBUY-REFER","source":["CT4107_Notes_05_Full_Life_Cycle_Use_Case","CT4107_Notes_03_Segmentation_Beachhead_TAM"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definition and purpose","items":["Understand and describe **how your product fits into the persona's workflow** across the whole life cycle (also called the **Customer Journey**).","Why: gives information for later steps and helps the team see **potential barriers to adoption** from a sales perspective.","\"The best product doesn't always win: it may be the product the customer is aware of.\"","Take the **customer's view of the end-to-end journey**, not only the touchpoints where you interact (McKinsey).","How the end user actually uses the product is the most complex part: use bullets, diagrams, flowcharts, pictures.","Result: a shared understanding in the team before the product is designed."]},{"heading":"The ten stages","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"row","steps":["1 Need","2 Find","3 Analyse","4 Acquire","5 Pay","6 Install","7 Use","8 Value","9 Buy more","10 Refer"]},"caption":"Ten stages of the full life-cycle use case"},{"type":"table","head":["#","Stage (question)","Helps us to..."],"rows":[["1","Awareness and need: how does the customer know they have a problem? What is the catalyst to act?","Develop solution and value proposition"],["2","Find: how do they search for and find your solution?","Develop go-to-market strategy and cost of customer acquisition (COCA)"],["3","Analyse: how do they assess value and compare competition to decide?","Develop minimum viable business product, value proposition, competitive positioning"],["4","Acquire: how do they get the product?","Develop business model and pricing"],["5","Pay: how do they pay?","Develop business model and pricing"],["6","Install / set up","Develop solution and value proposition"],["7","Use and get value (includes support)","Develop solution and value proposition; customer service"],["8","Determine value gained","Competitive positioning, satisfaction, iterate solution"],["9","Buy more","Go-to-market, business model, pricing, expansion plan, customer lifetime value"],["10","Tell others / spread awareness","Go-to-market strategy, incentives, word of mouth"]]},{"type":"note","text":"The slides show two slightly different splits: one counts \"Acquire and pay\" as a single stage and adds \"Receive support\" as stage 8; the template groups acquire and pay separately. State the ten stages you use and cover support under Use."}]},{"heading":"Template (fill for each stage)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Stage","Action","Who involved","When","Where","How","Notes"],"rows":[["1 Need","How does she determine the need and what triggers action?","","","","",""],["2 Find","How does she discover options?","","","","",""],["...","...","","","","",""],["10 Refer","How does she tell others?","","","","",""]]},"For every stage answer: **who** is involved, **when**, **where**, **how**, plus other relevant details; use sketches and flowcharts for the use stage."]},{"heading":"Worked mini-example: smart rack in a high-end gym (owner as buyer, member as end user)","items":["1 Need: a member is injured and cancels; owner sees rising injury claims (catalyst: insurance renewal).","2 Find: owner searches at fitness trade shows and asks the gym-chain network.","3 Analyse: compares with a personal-trainer model and basic video apps; asks for a pilot.","4-5 Acquire and pay: leases racks through the chain head office; monthly fee.","6 Install: technician mounts camera and rack in one afternoon; staff briefing.","7 Use: member lifts, a display gives instant form feedback, data synced to their app.","8 Value: owner sees fewer injury reports and better retention in the monthly report.","9 Buy more: more racks and the nutrition module for other branches.","10 Refer: owner presents results at the chain meeting; members post progress on social media."]},{"heading":"McKinsey journey actions and past examples","items":["The six McKinsey actions for customer journeys (identify journeys from the customer's view, understand how they navigate touchpoints, anticipate needs, learn what works, prioritise gaps, fix root causes) are listed under the market-segmentation topic; use them as a checklist when drawing the use case.","Examples of full life-cycle use cases from previous years on the slides: Food Locker, SafeSip, The Crew, TFI Live, FlexFusion.","Template layout: two tables (stages 1-5, stages 6-10), each stage with rows Action, Who is involved, When, Where, How, Notes. Stage actions: 1 determine need and the catalyst to take action; 2 find out about options; 3 analyse options; 4 acquire the product; 5 pay; 6 install or set up; 7 use and get value; 8 determine the value gained; 9 buy more; 10 tell others."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["List and explain the stages of the full life-cycle use case for a given idea.","Why is it called \"full life cycle\"? (Because the journey continues after purchase: use, value, rebuy, referral.)","How does each stage inform later steps (pricing, COCA, go-to-market, CLV)?","Where could barriers to adoption arise?"]}],"pitfalls":["Stopping at \"purchase\": the use, value, rebuy and referral stages carry marks.","Missing who/when/where/how detail; one-line answers.","Describing the company's sales process instead of the customer's view.","Not tying the stages to the persona (use her name and context)."]},{"id":"end-user-profile","title":"End-user profile / ideal customer profile (Step 3)","priority":8,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium-high","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**The End-User Profile describes the narrowly defined group in the beachhead market: demographics, psychographics, proxy products, watering holes, day in the life and weighted priorities (summing to 100).**","mnemonic":"D-P-P-W-D-P: Demographics, Psychographics, Proxy products, Watering holes, Day in the life, Priorities","source":["CT4107_Notes_03_Segmentation_Beachhead_TAM","CT4107_Notes_04_Customer_Persona"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definition and purpose","items":["A **customer profile (ideal customer profile)** describes the type of buyer/user you should target: the characteristics of the people most likely to buy and to get a lot of value from the product.","Goal: a description of a **narrowly defined subset of end users with similar characteristics and similar needs**.","Why: (1) identify, describe and segment customers by personalities, buying habits and behaviours, (2) keep the focus on the **end user**, (3) deepen understanding of the primary customer, (4) feed the **TAM calculation**.","Build it from **primary market research** (interviews, observation, surveys). Focus on data you can use to segregate customers statistically or gather easily by survey.","It is \"not comprehensive\" but points in the right direction: a **journey to specificity**. Build the company around the customer's needs, not your interests and capabilities."]},{"heading":"Template (six parts from the lecture)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Part","What to write","Example (gym lifter)"],"rows":[["Demographics","Gender, age, income, geography, job title, education, marital status...","Male/female 25-45, urban, mid-to-high income"],["Psychographics","Aspirations, fears, motivators, hobbies, values, habits, personality","Wants visible progress; fears injury; data-driven"],["Proxy products","Other products they own and value (high correlation with your target)","Fitness watch, protein subscription, tracking app"],["Watering holes","Places/associations/online platforms, ranked by priority and intensity","Gym, Reddit/YouTube lifting channels, Instagram"],["Day in the life","A day, and what is going on in their head","Training at 6pm, logs sets in a notepad, worries about form"],["Priorities","Their priorities with weights adding to 100","Avoid injury 35, gain strength 30, save time 20, cost 15"]]},"Also useful: what media they consume, their reason for purchase, what makes them special and identifiable, their story."]},{"heading":"Profile vs persona","items":["Profile = description of the **average** (typical) customer group; an \"average customer\" does not actually exist.","Persona (Step 5) = one **specific, fictional but representative** person who best exemplifies the profile (see persona topic)."]},{"heading":"Why proxy products and watering holes matter","items":["**Proxy products** let you find and recognise customers without asking: owning X predicts needing your product.","**Watering holes** tell you where to reach them cheaply: marketing channels, events, communities (links to channels in the BMC).","**Weighted priorities** tell you what to quantify in the value proposition (priority #1 becomes the QVP metric)."]},{"heading":"Lecture examples of profiles","items":["Slide wording: the profile gives **categories and ranges, not a person**. Collect data you can use to segregate customers statistically or gather easily by survey. Step 3 is also called the **target customer definition**.","Target customer example (online-activity index chart): audience = males 25-34 (index 139 versus 100 for average), with interests such as females (70%) and sports (50%).",{"type":"table","head":["Ride-sharing sample profile","Value"],"rows":[["Gender","Male, female"],["Age","17-40 years old"],["Region","East of Ireland"],["Occupation","Student, young professional, internal migrant, middle management"],["Social level","Medium, high"],["Characteristics","Has a smartphone"],["User category","Early adopters, technologically advanced"],["Other","Active users of social networks, outgoing personalities"]]},"TechTarget profile format: a short scenario then blocks for demographics, interests/attitudes, behavioural, preferred content. Its three examples: suburban online shopper (early 40s, two children, income about USD 55k), spouse-to-be (late 20s, never married), college student (19, income under USD 14k).","HubSpot basic-information format: demographics, products/services used, customer benefits, customer pain points. monday.com format: age, location, hobbies, income, favourite products, visit pattern.",{"type":"table","head":["Urban Cake Shop abridged profile","Morning commuters","Seasonal celebrators","One-time weddings"],"rows":[["Description","Come often, spend little; most common customer","Middle-aged matriarchs; smaller segment but biggest source of revenue","Tiny share of customers but about 15% of revenue"],["Age range","25-45","35-55","26-42"],["Most important to them","Fast service, fresh food and coffee","Unique seasonal treats that make celebrations special","Unique flavours, attentive service, delivery option"],["How to market","Sidewalk promotions, loyalty rewards, email coupons","Email marketing with seasonal promotions; show creations on Instagram","Targeted Facebook ads; wedding newsletter; Instagram and Pinterest posts"],["Preferred channels","Email, loyalty app","Email, Instagram, Facebook, direct mail","Email, Facebook, Pinterest"]]},"Fourth Urban Cake Shop group on the slide: casual drop-ins (novelty cakes, tea/coffee)."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Create an end-user profile for a named beachhead (fill the six parts).","Explain how the profile is used in later steps (TAM, persona, QVP, channels).","Difference between demographic and psychographic data; between profile and persona."]}],"pitfalls":["Writing a profile of the organisation or the economic buyer rather than the end user.","Only demographics, no psychographics, proxy products or priorities.","Priority weights that do not add to 100 or are all \"important\".","Invented details with no research behind them."]},{"id":"product-spec-mvbp","title":"High-level product specification and MVBP (Steps 7 and 22)","priority":9,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium-high","marks":"6-10 marks","summary":"**Define the product visually and from the customer's view before building anything: benefits not features, no price, a living document (e.g. a brochure); the MVBP later is the minimum product where the customer gets value, pays, and gives feedback.**","mnemonic":"DON'T BUILD YET: customer view, benefits, visual, no price, keep testing","source":["CT4107_Notes_07_Product_Spec_QVP","CT4107_Notes_09_Business_Model_Canvas"],"sections":[{"heading":"What the high-level product specification is (Step 7)","items":["A **visual representation of your product**: images and descriptions showing how it operates and, above all, **how it adds value for the beachhead market by addressing their main pain points**.","Focuses on the **benefits created by the features**.","Can be images, drawings, PowerPoint or short videos; one way is to build a **product brochure**.","Includes where to buy or find out more."]},{"heading":"Rules (the five commandments)","items":["**Do not build anything yet: define it first.**","Define it from the **customer's perspective**, not yours.","Talk about **benefits, not features**.","Do not drown them in detail; **make it visual**.","**Do not mention price**: far too early.","It is a **living document**: test, validate, iterate."]},{"heading":"Brochure checklist (from the golf-package example)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Element","What it contains"],"rows":[["Name and tagline","Memorable product name and a one-line promise (e.g. HELMET: haptic cycling safety; SLIM: simple liquid inventory management)"],["Visual of the product / journey","Picture or simple flow showing how it works"],["Primary benefit aligned with persona #1 priority","Visually dominant headline (e.g. \"More mid-week bookings, more revenue from capacity you already have\")"],["Two additional benefits","e.g. reach new customers; launch with less effort"],["Sense of magnitude","Pilot targets to validate, e.g. +10-20% mid-week bookings; not guarantees until tested"],["Clear call to action","e.g. \"Join the pilot: book a 30-minute planning session\""]]},"Position one clear message; avoid presenting the service as a broad marketplace."]},{"heading":"MVBP: minimum viable business product (Step 22)","items":["The **minimum viable business product** is the smallest version that does three things:","1. The **customer gets value** out of using the product.","2. The **customer pays** for the product.","3. The product is enough to **start the customer feedback loop**, so the customer helps you iterate towards a better product.","Difference from \"MVP\": it must be a viable **business** (customers pay), not just a technical prototype.","Link: the high-level spec (Step 7) is the vision; the MVBP is the first thing you actually build and sell, informed by the QVP and key assumptions."]},{"heading":"Where these steps sit","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":["Persona (5)","Life-cycle use case (6)","High-level spec (7)","QVP (8)","Core (10)","Competitive position (11)","MVBP (22)"]},"caption":"Order of the customer-facing steps"}]},{"heading":"More from the Step 7 slides","items":["Cartoon on the Step 7 slide: 'a picture is worth a thousand words': people interpret a text description differently, a visual removes the ambiguity.","Brochure examples shown: **H.E.L.M.E.T** (Haptic Environment Listener and Motion Event Telemetry: smart cycling helmet with ultrasonic sensor array, haptic feedback and heads-up display; customers: commuters, leisure cyclists, tech enthusiasts), **SLIM** (liquid inventory management: a scale measures stock, software updates inventory, cloud analytics, 'saves time, money and stress') and **Biegando** (app that brings local walking and running events and trails to your phone; 'first year free for every student'). Each has a name, tagline, benefit headline, simple visuals and contact details.","A template ('Product alignment with persona') links each of the persona's top priorities (1, 2, 3) to: how you will deliver a new level of value on that priority; what **features**; what **functions**; what **benefits**. The brochure itself leads with the benefits.","Do not focus on technology, features and functions; answer what potential customers want to gain when they buy and ask themselves 'what value do I get out of this product?'.","Step 22 slide ('minimum viable product'): the cartoon says the customer wanted the Taj Mahal but you first build what gets you the oxygen to keep going: real customer feedback and cash."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Describe what a high-level product specification is and the rules for writing it.","Write a brochure outline for a given idea (headline benefit, two more, magnitude, call to action).","Define MVBP and explain its three conditions; compare with MVP.","Why should you not mention price or build yet?"]}],"pitfalls":["Listing technical features and architecture instead of benefits.","Mentioning price or building a prototype in the spec step.","Magnitude claims stated as guarantees instead of hypotheses to validate.","MVBP without a paying customer or without a feedback loop."]},{"id":"core-competitive-position","title":"Define your core, moat and competitive position (Steps 10-11)","priority":10,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium-high","marks":"6-10 marks","summary":"**The core is the one thing you do better than anyone and protect at all costs (the moat: unique, grows, important); the competitive position chart plots you against alternatives on the persona's top two priorities, including the do-nothing option.**","mnemonic":"MOAT = Unique, Grows, Important; chart = two priorities, good in the top right","source":["CT4107_Notes_07_Product_Spec_QVP","CT4107_Notes_09_Business_Model_Canvas"],"sections":[{"heading":"Core and competitive moat (Step 10)","items":["Your **core** gives **sustainable competitive advantage**; you invest your resources in it to do better than anyone else and it is the source of your unique selling proposition over time.","You do not want to create a product and market and then be unable to hold them: you need something **hard to replicate: the \"moat\"**.","Competitive (economic) moat, popularised by Warren Buffett: a business's ability to keep competitive advantages that protect long-term profits and market share.","Forms of core: an **algorithm** based on unique knowledge/research you own; a **process** (e.g. manufacturing); **expertise** (hard to acquire, difficult to put in a product, and potentially not unique).","Three dimensions of a good core: **Unique** (hard for others to duplicate), **Grows** (strengthens over time relative to competitors), **Important** (ties directly to the quantified value proposition)."]},{"heading":"Examples (lecture exercise)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Company","Value proposition","Core"],"rows":[["IKEA","Low cost, good designs","Logistics, vendor management"],["DHL","Fast, reliable delivery","Operational excellence"],["Google","World's information","Evolved from search to knowing everything (data)"],["Amazon","The everything store","Huge experience in e-commerce services"],["Apple","Instant productivity","User experience: consistently delivering quality experiences"]]},"Worksheet: write your value proposition, list 4-6 candidate moats from strongest to weakest, and decide your core."]},{"heading":"Competitive position chart (Step 11): recipe","items":["1. Decide the persona's **top 2 priorities** (X and Y).","2. Draw axes; split each in half. Write the priority name on the axis; near the origin write the **bad state** (e.g. low reliability), further out the **good state** (high).","3. Plot your product and the main competitors (current and future) **and the customer's do-nothing / status-quo option**.","4. You want to sit in the **top-right (good on both)**, separated from alternatives.","5. Compare with the Quantified Value Proposition: priority #1 is measured on the x-axis.","Lecture example: SunSpring is plotted on efficiency (x) vs reliability (y) against \"with battery\" and \"no battery\" alternatives; its value proposition is increased efficiency, flexibility, mobility, reliability and operational simplicity compared with competitors.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"scatter","data":{"title":"Competitive position (illustrative)","xLabel":"Efficiency (persona priority #1)","yLabel":"Reliability (priority #2)","xMin":0,"xMax":100,"yMin":0,"yMax":100,"series":[{"name":"Alternatives","points":[[30,80],[75,30],[20,20]]}],"points":[{"x":85,"y":85,"label":"OUR PRODUCT"},{"x":30,"y":80,"label":"Alt 1"},{"x":75,"y":30,"label":"Alt 2"},{"x":20,"y":20,"label":"Do nothing"}]},"caption":"Own product top-right; alternatives and do-nothing elsewhere"}]},{"heading":"How core and position link to other steps","items":["Competitive position chart supports the **core**; the **resource plan** supports building the core.","Axes come from the persona priorities; the QVP measures value on priority #1.","**SWOT** is a complementary analysis: internal **S**trengths and **W**eaknesses, external **O**pportunities and **T**hreats (lecture example: Apple, strong brand and margin vs high prices; AI and green tech opportunities vs trade war risk)."]},{"heading":"Core: more from the slides","items":["Slide title: **secret sauce** (castle and moat picture). 'What makes for a great core?' = Unique, Grows, Important.","Core worksheet: (1) what is your value proposition? (2) list your moats from strongest to weakest (up to six); (3) what is your core? Orbit-style output: value proposition, team assets (prioritised), proposed moats (strongest to weakest), potential cores, and a recommended core with why it is unique, why it matters to customers, how it can grow over time.","Link the core to superior value with a table: core capability, customer benefit, why competitors may not match it. The key work is to connect customer value to defensible assets, practical moats and a core that strengthens as the business grows. The slide 'how does your core map to what your customer really wants?' stresses the Important dimension.","Metaphor: **customer's nirvana** is across a river; the bridge to it is built by your core and is only passable to you; competitors and the as-is state are stuck on the near bank."]},{"heading":"Competitive position chart: more detail","items":["Chart template: target customer priorities 1 and 2; each axis runs from terrible/bad to great/good; a star marks you; place each alternative and the do-nothing option. Slide inconsistency: the written recipe puts priority #1 on the x-axis, the printed template puts priority #1 on the y-axis. Either is fine if labelled; the lecture example SunSpring plots efficiency on x and reliability on y.","SunSpring example (solar-powered water filtering system): competitors AquaSun, Spectra Watermakers, WorldWater and Solar Technologies, Blue Spring; reliability axis split into 'with battery' (low) and 'no battery' (high); SunSpring top-right.",{"type":"table","head":["Orbit-style competitive table","Priority 1 position","Priority 2 position","Rationale"],"rows":[["Your venture (golf mid-week packages)","High","High","Upper-right target: specialised solution"],["Golf travel agencies and tour operators","Medium-High","Medium","Package golf travel but favour established destinations"],["Online travel agencies","Medium","Low-Medium","Broad distribution, but operators build packages themselves"],["Golf tee-time marketplaces","Medium","Low","Fill tee times only, a narrow inventory problem"]]},"Follow-up questions in that format: are you in the upper-right corner? why are some competitors close? what is the principal competitive gap to overcome? how should the position be communicated? Slide exercise: chart the competitive position of **Airbnb** (decide top 2 priorities, compare with main competitors)."]},{"heading":"SWOT: lecture definitions and four company examples","items":["SWOT = internal factors (**Strengths**, **Weaknesses**) and external factors (**Opportunities**, **Threats**); strengths and opportunities are positive, weaknesses and threats negative.","Prompts: Strengths = things your company does well, qualities that separate you from competitors, internal resources (skilled staff), tangible assets (IP, capital, proprietary technology). Weaknesses = things your company lacks or competitors do better, resource limitations, unclear USP. Opportunities = underserved markets, few competitors in your area, emerging need for your products, press coverage. Threats = emerging competitors, changing regulation, negative press, changing customer attitudes.",{"type":"table","head":["Company","Strengths","Weaknesses","Opportunities","Threats"],"rows":[["Ryanair","Point-to-point flights; young uniform fleet; cheap outsourced labour; strong marketing model; innovator in cost reduction","Poor customer service; poor working conditions; bad public image","Market-share expansion; good relations with unions (e.g. BALPA); website and modern technology; competition committee","EU regulation; national laws; substitute transport; criticism of budget airlines; legal issues; fuel-price fluctuation; competition domination"],["Apple","World's strongest brand; very high margin; global reach; expanding consumer base","High prices; tracking allegations; software incompatibility","Growing customer base; green technology; artificial intelligence","Increasing competition; market penetration; risk of a significant trade war"],["Airbnb","Pleasant experience; affordability; uniqueness; accessible to all","Customer dissatisfaction; lawsuits; review dependency","Growing urbanisation; easy correspondence; competitive advantage; celebrity affiliation","Higher competitiveness; pandemic outrage; contagious virus"],["Amazon","Customer service; IT; efficient supply chain and logistics; huge global brand; early e-commerce retailer; diversified range","Diversified range (brand confusion); losses from free shipping","Public-sector collaboration; joint ventures (e.g. Toys-R-Us); entered China 2004 (Joyo.com); growth in US online sales","Brand competition (Kindle vs iPad); international competitors; poor publicity; currency fluctuation"]]}]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Define core/moat and give three properties; give examples for known companies.","Draw and explain a competitive position chart for a given idea (with do-nothing).","Distinguish value proposition, QVP, USP and core.","Do a SWOT for the startup."]}],"pitfalls":["Choosing a core that is easy to copy (e.g. \"good customer service\" with no mechanism).","Axes that are features rather than customer priorities.","Forgetting the do-nothing option as a competitor.","Core not tied to the QVP (not important to the customer)."]},{"id":"business-model-basics","title":"Business model, revenue model and business plan","priority":11,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium","marks":"4-8 marks","summary":"**A business model describes how a company creates, delivers and captures value (who, what, when, where, why); the revenue model is how it makes money (pricing, channels); a business plan is the longer, detailed document of strategy and financial forecasts.**","mnemonic":"MODEL = how value is created, delivered, captured; PLAN = years of detail","source":["CT4107_Notes_09_Business_Model_Canvas","CT4107_Notes_09b_What_Is_a_Business_Model_Canvas"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions","items":["**Business plan**: a plan for the successful operation of a business, identifying sources of revenue, the intended customer base, products and details of financing; spells out strategy and expected financial performance over a number of years.","**Business model**: the broader framework outlining how a company **creates, delivers and captures value** (the \"value capture framework\"); answers who, what, when, where, why.","**Revenue model / revenue stream**: the method by which the business makes money, including how you price and which sales channels you choose (ads, subscriptions, transaction fees...).","Developing a model first (rather than a plan) lets you gather customer feedback sooner and adjust; a plan written later is more realistic."]},{"heading":"Comparison","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","Business model","Business plan"],"rows":[["Focus","How value is created, delivered, captured","Strategy, operations, forecasts"],["Length","One page (canvas)","Detailed document"],["Time horizon","Current/proposed logic","Several years of financials"],["When","Early, to test and iterate","Later, once the model is clearer"]]}]},{"heading":"Business model innovation examples","items":["**Google**: before it, search engines fitted as many banner ads on a page as possible and charged as much as possible. Google used simple text ads targeted by search keywords, so advertisers got better data on ad effectiveness: a new value-capture framework.","**Groupon**: two-sided platform earning a percentage fee on transactions, with ancillary services.","**Ordr**: a platform that makes queueing for payment easy (idea used in the lecture to illustrate a model).","Types of revenue stream to mention (general knowledge, not listed on the slides except ads and subscriptions): advertising, subscription, transaction fee, one-off sales, licensing, freemium, leasing."]},{"heading":"More from the Step 15 slides","items":["Cartoon: a seesaw comparing time spent on **value-creation innovation** with time spent on **value-capture innovation**; the message is to balance them (most teams neglect how they capture value).","Slide wheel for 'business model vs revenue model': core value, competencies, target customer, product and services, marketing, management, distribution, cost; 'the business model includes the revenue model'.","Step 15 definition on the slide (business plan wording): a plan for the successful operation of a business, identifying sources of revenue, the intended customer base, products and details of financing."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Business model vs business plan vs revenue model.","Explain how a named company makes money and what is novel about its model."]}],"pitfalls":["Treating revenue model and business model as the same thing: revenue is only one part (one block of nine).","Describing what a company sells but not how value is captured."]},{"id":"primary-market-research","title":"Primary market research (PMR) methods","priority":12,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium","marks":"4-8 marks","summary":"**PMR starts now, never ends and underlies almost every one of the 24 steps: interviews, observation, immersion, user tests, focus groups, diary studies and landing-page tests, iterated with people skills plus data analysis.**","mnemonic":"ASK, WATCH, BECOME, TEST (and never stop)","source":["CT4107_Notes_03_Segmentation_Beachhead_TAM","CT4107_Notes_04_Customer_Persona"],"sections":[{"heading":"Principles","items":["PMR (primary market research) = you collect first-hand evidence from real potential customers yourself.","It **starts now, never ends, and underlies nearly every step** of the 24 steps.","Successful PMR is a continual iteration of **engaging people (people skills) + common-sense data analysis**.","Treat AI-tool output as unproven hypotheses to validate through PMR."]},{"heading":"Methods","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Method","What it is","Tip / example"],"rows":[["Customer interviews","One-to-one conversations","Open-ended questions; let the subject lead; structure for consistency"],["Contextual interview","Interview where the product will be used","Sleep product: interview at home, house tour, discuss problems"],["Observational research","Watch behaviour in context","See workarounds users do not mention"],["Immersion","Become the user","Team uses 4 competing CAD programs on the same parts and documents learning"],["Secondary research","Existing reports and data","Cheap background and sizing; not a substitute for PMR"],["User tests / usability benchmark","Give a task, watch them struggle, learn why","Reveals real problems"],["Longitudinal diary studies","Users log experiences over days/weeks","Learn what 1-2 hour sessions miss"],["Focus groups","Targeted questions to a group","Beware group-think"],["Landing page test","Page with call to action to test interest or purchase intent","Email capture or \"card not charged until we ship\"; tools like unbounce.com; conversion metrics (vaporware test)"],["User-driven innovation","Users take part in creating the products they buy","Co-design"]]}]},{"heading":"Contextual interview flow (sleep product example)","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"row","steps":["Warm-up chat","\"Tell us the story of sleep in your house\"","House tour","Discuss problems","Probe expectations","Show idea only if time and asked"]},"caption":"Show the product idea last, and only if they ask"}]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Describe three PMR methods suited to validating a beachhead segment.","How would you test demand without building the product? (landing page, concierge/prototype.)","Why is primary research better than relying on desk research or AI output?"]}],"pitfalls":["Leading questions or pitching the product during interviews.","Treating a few friendly opinions as validation.","Doing PMR once instead of continuously."]},{"id":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","title":"Disciplined Entrepreneurship: the 24 steps and course deliverables","priority":13,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium-high","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**Aulet's 24-step MIT framework is a teachable process for building a startup around six themes; the course deliverables follow the early steps: idea, segmentation/beachhead/profile, persona and use case, QVP/spec, BMC, video pitch.**","mnemonic":"6 themes: WHO customer, WHAT for them, HOW they buy, MONEY, BUILD, SCALE","source":["CT4107_Notes_01_Introduction","CT4107_Notes_03_Segmentation_Beachhead_TAM","CT4107_Notes_07_Product_Spec_QVP"],"sections":[{"heading":"What it is","items":["Disciplined Entrepreneurship (Bill Aulet, MIT) turns entrepreneurship into a **process**: six questions and 24 steps (the \"DE canvas\").","Benefits: raises odds of success, structures the startup, gives a common language, exposes weak points.","Six themes: **Who is your customer? What can you do for your customer? How does your customer acquire your product? How do you make money? How do you design and build your product? How do you scale?**"]},{"heading":"The 24 steps","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Theme","Steps"],"rows":[["Who is your customer?","1 Market segmentation, 2 Select a beachhead market, 3 Build an end-user profile, 4 Calculate the TAM size for the beachhead market, 5 Profile the persona for the beachhead market, 9 Identify your next 10 customers"],["What can you do for your customer?","6 Full life-cycle use case, 7 High-level product specification, 8 Quantify the value proposition, 10 Define your core, 11 Chart your competitive position"],["How does your customer acquire your product?","12 Determine the decision-making unit (DMU) of the customer, 13 Map the process to acquire a paying customer, 18 Map the sales process to acquire a customer"],["How do you make money off your product?","15 Design a business model, 16 Set your pricing framework, 17 Calculate the lifetime value (LTV) of an acquired customer, 19 Calculate the cost of customer acquisition (COCA)"],["How do you design and build your product?","20 Identify key assumptions, 21 Test key assumptions, 22 Define the minimum viable business product (MVBP), 23 Show that \"the dogs will eat the dog food\""],["How do you scale your business?","14 Calculate the TAM size for follow-on markets, 24 Develop a product plan"]]},{"type":"note","text":"The lecture works through steps 1-11 in detail and the business model (step 15) via the BMC. Steps 12-24 are named on the slide map; know their names and their place in the sequence."}]},{"heading":"Course deliverables (team portfolio)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Week","Deliverable"],"rows":[["2","Idea generation"],["4","Market segmentation, beachhead market analysis, end-user profiling"],["6","Customer persona, life-cycle use case"],["8","Quantified value proposition, high-level specification"],["10","Business model canvas / business plan"],["12","Video pitch (submit to Student Entrepreneur Awards)"]]},"Also competitive positioning and mapping the product acquisition process; attendance and participation. Teams of **2 to 4**; assessed on quality of work, collaboration, design and justification of choices.","Teams keep an online portfolio documenting their journey."]},{"heading":"Using AI tools (MIT Orbit / AI Jetpack)","items":["The class trials MIT Orbit, whose AI assistant fills out the 24 steps from your idea.","AI output is treated as **unproven hypotheses** to validate or invalidate with your own research.","Keep an **AI Interaction Log** (exact prompts, weaknesses of responses, iterations forced).","Append a **Delta Log** for each DE step using Orbit: how you cross-referenced, audited and corrected the AI assumptions (users, behaviour, market size, data, technology)."]},{"heading":"Idea pitch before step 1","items":["Pick your best idea: what it does, the problem solved, customers, value, competitors, USP, who/what is needed.","**Elevator pitch** components: the **benefit** (what the customer gets, not the product), the **differentiator**, the **ask** (call to action, e.g. another meeting or feedback)."]},{"heading":"Extra from the slides","items":["The 24 steps are drawn as a snaking game board starting at step 1 (market segmentation) and ending at step 24 (develop a product plan). Step numbers follow the order of work, but the six themes group them differently (for example step 9 sits in 'who is your customer' and step 14 in 'how do you scale').","Slide: 'What's one of the most important components of a new commercial enterprise?' Answer shown: **paying customers**.","Slide: for the pitch, pick your best idea and answer: what it does, what problem it solves, who the customers are, what value, who the competitors are, your USP, who or what is needed to make it real.","Slide rules for AI use: from step 1 you may use the AI-assisted tool or do the work yourselves in the workbooks; either way validate all assumptions and claims with references; 'it is foolish to assume the AI-generated analysis is trustworthy'.","Team formation slide: 'Which team would you prefer to work in? Think about key success factors in high-performing teams you have been in'. Work on an idea the team is excited about; ideation and team formation determine your experience; change the idea mid-semester if it dead-ends.","The class needs **Idea, Team, Process**."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Outline the 24-step process and justify why it is useful.","Order the steps from segmentation to MVBP and say what each produces.","How should AI output be treated in the process? (hypotheses, delta log.)"]}],"pitfalls":["Mixing up step order (profile before TAM, persona before use case).","Treating AI output as fact.","Forgetting that the process is iterative: change the idea if it dead-ends."]},{"id":"intro-innovation","title":"Innovation, invention, entrepreneurship and the entrepreneurial process","priority":14,"tier":"possible","freq":"est. medium","marks":"5-10 marks (essay)","summary":"**Innovation = invention x commercialisation: an invention is a creative idea, an innovation makes it feasible and turns it into a product or service customers value; entrepreneurship is creating a new venture that produces something valuable and is economically sustainable.**","mnemonic":"INNOVATION = INVENTION x COMMERCIALISATION; venture must be economically sustainable","source":["CT4107_Notes_01_Introduction","CT4107_Notes_02a_Week2_Part1"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions","items":["**Innovation**: using new technology and new ways of thinking to add value to an existing idea or product and make substantial changes in society; \"new, organic value creation by applying creativity, in-depth relationships with consumers and customers, and new thinking\".","Also: executing an idea which addresses a specific challenge and achieves value for both company and customer; implementation or creation of something new that has realised value to others.","**Invention** = a creative idea. **Innovation** = makes the idea feasible and turns it into a product/service that satisfies customers' needs.",{"type":"formula","label":"Lecture equation","text":"Innovation = Invention x Commercialisation"},"If either factor is zero, innovation is zero: a brainstorm with dozens of ideas shows creativity but \"there is no innovation until something gets implemented\".","Innovation is the cornerstone of sustained economic growth and prosperity (OECD).","**Entrepreneurship / creation of new businesses**: the formation of a new venture that produces a product/offering that creates some value to make it **economically sustainable**."]},{"heading":"Innovation is more than new products","items":["It can concern new markets, new marketing methods, new processes or new business models.","It often builds on **old ideas and resources**: Scotch Tape (1930, industrial packaging) was later resold as Sellotape (tape plus dispenser) as an office commodity.","It can be **accidental**: in 1968 Dr Spencer Silver at 3M tried to make a super-strong adhesive and instead made a low-tack reusable pressure-sensitive adhesive (later Post-it notes).","Innovators watch for **changes** that hint at future opportunities. Problems lead to questions lead to innovations.","Product innovation often grows around a **core technology**: the internal combustion engine led to cars, planes, boat engines; then fuel injection, turbocharging, rotary engines. Image-sensor core led to digital cameras, DSLRs and AI subject tracking."]},{"heading":"Existing business vs new innovation-based business","items":[{"type":"table","head":["","Existing business","Innovation-based new business"],"rows":[["Model","Proven model; executes and optimises","Model unproven; must be discovered and validated"],["Risk","Lower","High; \"trying is the first step towards failure\""],["Customers","Existing","Must be found, starting with one beachhead market"],["Method","Operate efficiently","Experiment, iterate, pivot (disciplined process)"]]}]},{"heading":"The entrepreneurial process","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"row","steps":["Identify an opportunity","Establish a vision","Persuade others","Gather resources","Create new venture, product or market"],"loopBack":"Change and adapt with time"},"caption":"Entrepreneurial process from the lecture"}]},{"heading":"Is entrepreneurship teachable?","items":["Yes: per Bill Aulet (MIT) it is \"not magic, it is work, a process, and a process can be taught\"; it is accessible (anyone can do it) yet produces unique products.","A teachable craft, **not innate talent**; apprenticeship model: theory merged with practice.","A process (disciplined entrepreneurship) substantially increases odds of success, gives structure to a critical element of starting a company, a **common language**, and identifies questions and weak points.","Three things for the class: **Idea, Team, Process**. Take team formation seriously; be willing to change a dead-end idea."]},{"heading":"Extra lecture content: sources of innovation, ways to start, science-craft-art","items":["**Drucker's 7 sources of innovation** (slide): (1) the unexpected (surprise success or failure), (2) incongruities (discrepancy between what is and what should be), (3) process needs (a bottleneck or weak link in a critical process), (4) industry and market structure changes, (5) demographics (moving populations, changing age, education, disposable income), (6) changes in perception, meaning and mood (views of life, how people feel about things), (7) new knowledge (new technologies and discoveries). Innovators attend to changes that give clues to future opportunities.","Ways to start a new venture (slide cartoon): 'I have a technological breakthrough', 'I have an idea', 'I have a passion'.","'The truth' slide: entrepreneurship sits on a spectrum between **science** and **art**, nearest to **craft** (hence teachable). Slide jokes about the 'entrepreneurship gene' to make the point it is not innate.","Course objective: 'primary goal is not to give a fish but to teach how to fish' (Chinese proverb): learn the process.","'Everyone has the ability to innovate': problems lead to questions which lead to innovations. Businesses that invest in creativity: more likely to raise employee productivity by 73% and foster innovation by 83% (slide figures).","Post-it story dates: invented 1968 (Dr Spencer Silver, 3M); slide shows 1979, then USA 1980 and EU 1981.","Where to look for ideas: emerging technology lists (WEF Top 10 Emerging Technologies of 2025, EY emerging technologies, medical futurist, transport trends) or search 'emerging technology trends in X'."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Distinguish invention, creativity and innovation, with an example.","Explain the equation Innovation = Invention x Commercialisation.","Outline the entrepreneurial process.","Argue whether entrepreneurship can be taught."]}],"pitfalls":["Equating innovation with invention or with \"a new gadget\".","No example (3M sticky note, Scotch to Sellotape, ICE core technology).","Forgetting that the venture must be economically sustainable."]},{"id":"windows-of-opportunity","title":"Windows of opportunity and triggers","priority":15,"tier":"possible","freq":"est. medium","marks":"4-6 marks","summary":"**A window of opportunity (WoO) is a time when the buyer is open to your offer; a trigger is a specific action you take inside it that creates urgency (discount, limited supply, time limit, free stuff, sales-rep interaction).**","mnemonic":"WINDOW = when they are open; TRIGGER = what you do to make them act NOW","source":["CT4107_Notes_09_Business_Model_Canvas"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions","items":["**Window of opportunity**: a time when your target end user, economic buyer, champion or influencer will be particularly open to considering your offering.","**Trigger**: within the window, a specific action you take to create urgency or a strong incentive for the target customer to act.","Illustration (general knowledge, not from the slides): lemonade sells on a hot day (window) but not in snow; even then you need a well-designed trigger."]},{"heading":"Examples","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Windows","Triggers"],"rows":[["Holiday season","Discount (expiring)"],["End of fiscal year, beginning of budgeting cycle","Limited supply / scarcity"],["Life transitions, change in leadership","Time-bounded offer"],["Crisis, or a competitor acquired (e.g. by Oracle)","Free stuff, e.g. free audit of customer's status"],["Start of school year (students)","Back-to-school sales, giveaways, trade-in programme"],["After a high-visibility security breach","Sales-rep interaction; avoid-disaster messaging"],["Valentine's Day","Jewellery discount"]]},"Expedia \"2 seats remaining at this price\" combines scarcity with a looking-at-flights window.","Use in the life-cycle use case: stage 1 catalyst to act; and in go-to-market planning."]},{"heading":"How to create a trigger (slide)","items":["Five trigger pictures on the slide: **expiring discount**, **time-limited offer**, **scarcity**, **free stuff**, **sales-rep interaction**. Written list: discount, limited supply, time bounded, community, avoid disaster.","Slide title: 'but you also need a well-designed trigger'. The windows list also includes life transitions, change in leadership and crisis."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Define WoO and trigger and give an example for a named startup.","Identify the window and the trigger in a scenario."]}],"pitfalls":["Treating a trigger as the window: window = timing/context; trigger = your action.","Triggers with no real incentive or urgency."]},{"id":"creativity-ideation","title":"Creativity and the 5-step creative process; defining the problem","priority":16,"tier":"possible","freq":"est. medium","marks":"5-12 marks","summary":"**Creativity = problem finding and solving with relevance, value and novelty; use the 5-step process (objective, data gathering, problem design, ideation, selection) and spend most time defining the problem.**","mnemonic":"Creativity = Find, Relevant, Valuable, Novel. Process: OBJECTIVE, DATA, PROBLEM, IDEAS, PICK","source":["CT4107_Notes_01_Introduction","CT4107_Notes_02a_Week2_Part1","CT4107_Notes_02b_Week2_Part2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Creativity (four elements)","items":["**Problem finding and solving**: creative people actively seek problems and imagine future situations.","**Relevance**: how well the solution actually solves the problem at hand.","**Value**: importance to the customer (and creator): is it useful?","**Novelty**: originality; a unique element, or combining concepts from different fields.","Creativity is different from innovation: ideas must be implemented. Businesses that invest in creativity report more productivity (73%) and innovation (83%)."]},{"heading":"Know the problem first","items":["Einstein (attributed): with one hour to save the world, spend 55 minutes defining the problem and 5 finding the solution.","Three ways to define the problem: **rephrase** it, **expose and challenge assumptions**, **find multiple perspectives**.","Rephrase: a Toyota executive asking for \"ways to increase productivity\" got blank stares, but \"ways to make your jobs easier\" produced a flood of ideas.","Challenge assumptions: \"restaurants have a menu\" (a restaurant where customers bring dish ideas for the chef).","Multiple perspectives: view \"increase sales\" from the customer's side (features worth paying more for).","Problem vs outcome: distinguish the problem (excessive waiting) from the outcome sought (better patient outcomes, more orders handled).","Analysis tools: **5 Whys**, **Root Cause Analysis**, **Fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram**.","\"Probable futures\" exercise: how has an area changed in 5 years, how will it change in the next 5, what problems offer opportunities, what products/services will emerge?"]},{"heading":"The 5-step creative process","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"row","steps":[{"label":"1 Objective","sub":"Goal / ideal state"},{"label":"2 Data gathering","sub":"Facts, feelings, questions"},{"label":"3 Problem design","sub":"Right problem?"},{"label":"4 Ideation","sub":"Many possibilities"},{"label":"5 Selection","sub":"Criteria, ruthless"}]},"caption":"Diverge and converge: data gathering and ideation diverge, then converge"},{"type":"table","head":["Step","What you do"],"rows":[["1 Objective","Define the goal (a state of being, the result of a solution), not the solution. Complete \"Wouldn't it be great if...\". Then list problems to solve for that ideal state, solutions needed, and where to find them."],["2 Data gathering","Divergent: collect facts (history, who is involved, what worked and failed, obstacles, restrictions) and feelings (how people feel now and should feel). Raise new questions. Convergent: tag each item Vital, Interesting or Insight; summarise the objective in a one-minute explanation."],["3 Problem design","Check that solving the stated problem leads to the objective. Is it too broad (too many solutions)? Too specific (only scratches the surface)? Move up or down the **ladder of abstraction** to design the right problem."],["4 Ideation","Generate as many **possibilities** (not solutions) as possible: idea webs, input exercises, story structure. Quantity first."],["5 Selection","Set selection criteria (\"the solution will work if it...\", \"will it...?\", \"does it...?\"), strengthen and combine ideas, make each actionable, then apply criteria ruthlessly."]]},{"type":"note","text":"General idea-generation process also given in lecture: define the problem, agree judging criteria, set restrictions; then research, insight, generate ideas, hone, test (example: unhappy train commuters)."}]},{"heading":"Good entrepreneurial business ideas (checklist)","items":["**Solve a problem**, find a market need, are customer-focused (not product-driven), target an identified **sizeable market segment**.","**Attractive** (demand), **Achievable**, **Durable**, **Value-creating**, **Safe**, **Affordable**.","**Faster / better / cheaper**, differentiated vs commodity, have a \"special sauce\".","Revenue stream; **sustainable** (market exists with purchase frequency); **scalable or replicable**; barriers to entry; growth potential; product pipeline; exit plan.","Thomas Edison exercise: you have invented the light bulb; plan a business: What is the problem? Who is the market? Who is the competition? What is the USP?"]},{"heading":"Good ideas grouped as in the lecture, plus the general idea process","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Heading on the slide","Attributes"],"rows":[["Market driven","Solves a problem; finds a market need; customer focused not product driven; targets an identified, sizeable market segment"],["Feasible","Attractive (there is demand), Achievable (can be done), Durable (it lasts), Value creating (worth something), Safe, Affordable"],["Unique","Faster/better/cheaper; differentiated versus commodity; has a special sauce"],["Fundable","Revenue stream; sustainable (market exists with frequency of purchase); scalable or replicable; barriers to entry; growth potential; product pipeline; exit plan"],["Innovative","Invention (totally new product/service/process); Extension (new use or application of an existing one); Duplication (replication of an existing concept); Synthesis (combining existing concepts or factors into a new formula)"]]},"Mnemonic for the grouping: **M-F-U-F-I**: Market driven, Feasible, Unique, Fundable, Innovative.","General process for generating ideas: define the problem, agree judging criteria, set restrictions; then **R**esearch, **I**nsight, **G**enerate ideas, **H**one, **T**est (spells RIGHT; example: unhappy train commuters).","Ideation slide goals: widen your potential ideas; refine those ideas for your new design; focus on user pain points; incorporate useful elements. In the 5-step pencil diagram: during ideation do not solve the problem, generate as many possibilities as possible and leave the judgement to the next stage; in selection be very specific about what constitutes a solution and be ruthless against the criteria.","Several ideation approaches share a lot: the 5-step creative process, IDEO, Systematic Inventive Thinking, SCAMPER. The IDEO, SIT and SCAMPER slides are marked **supplementary material** (worth knowing, helpful if stuck)."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Define creativity and its four elements; distinguish creativity and innovation.","Describe the 5-step creative process with an example.","Why spend more time on the problem than the solution?","List characteristics of a good business idea and assess a given idea against them."]}],"pitfalls":["Jumping to ideation (step 4) and skipping the objective and data steps.","Confusing a goal (state of being) with a solution.","Ideation that judges ideas too early: separate generation from selection.","Choosing ideas by gut feel instead of explicit selection criteria."]},{"id":"ideation-techniques","title":"Ideation techniques: SCAMPER, SIT, IDEO","priority":17,"tier":"low","freq":"est. medium-low (marked \"supplementary material\")","marks":"4-8 marks","summary":"**SCAMPER (Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Modify, Put to another use, Eliminate, Reverse) and SIT (Subtraction, Division, Multiplication, Unification, Attribute dependency) are systematic prompts for generating new ideas from existing products.**","mnemonic":"SCAMPER = S-C-A-M-P-E-R; SIT = S-D-M-U-A","source":["CT4107_Notes_02b_Week2_Part2"],"sections":[{"heading":"SCAMPER (7 prompts, apply to an existing product)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Letter","Prompt","Lecture example"],"rows":[["S Substitute","What can be replaced (components, materials, people)?","Playhouse windows: glass to plastic; plastic bottle to biodegradable PLA"],["C Combine","What can be merged (features, devices, steps)?","Phone + camera; sushi pizza"],["A Adapt","What can be adapted or borrowed from elsewhere?","Smartphone with interchangeable lenses (from cameras); cars with built-in wifi"],["M Modify (magnify, minify)","Change size, shape, colour, attribute; more or less of a feature","Baby sunglasses; folding/extending dining table"],["P Put to another use","Different use, market or user?","Coffee filters as face-mask filters; industrial drones for wind turbine inspection"],["E Eliminate","What can be removed or simplified?","Removing car cigarette lighters; simplifying an app's buttons"],["R Reverse / rearrange","Reverse the process or order","Fast food: pay then eat; investors pitching to project owners"]]},"Flask example: substitute a plastic cap, combine with artist designs, adapt a telescopic extension, modify with a rubber grip, put to another use (couples' detachable mug compartments), eliminate the handle, reverse (keep drinks cold).","Use SCAMPER questions as a checklist, then select with criteria."]},{"heading":"SIT: Systematic Inventive Thinking (the five tools, from the slides)","items":["Principle (slides): **innovate by starting with the solution and then work backwards to the problem** ('why would that be beneficial?'). Also called the **Function Follows Form** principle.","Method: (1) start with an existing situation, (2) apply one of the five thinking tools, (3) ask **'Should we do it and can we do it?'**.","Component = a physical or tangible thing you can see or touch. Attribute = a characteristic of the product or service (or of its surrounding environment and people).",{"type":"table","head":["Tool","Slide definition","Procedure from the slides","Slide examples"],"rows":[["Subtraction","Elimination of an essential component of a product (finding innovative solutions by removing an apparently essential component of an existing solution)","1 List all the internal components. 2 Remove a component. 3 Ask: should we do it and can we do it? 4 Modify the concept.","Exercise bicycle (a bicycle with the wheels removed), powdered soup, contact lens, child's highchair: \"what do they have in common?\" (each is a familiar product with an apparently essential part taken away)"],["Division","Separate the product or its components and then rearrange them back into a product","1 List the product's internal components. 2 Divide the product or one of its components (functionally, physically or preservationally).","Functionally (slide picture: water skiing), physically (slide picture: car dashboard), preservationally (a cake divided into cupcakes: each smaller unit preserves the characteristics of the whole)"],["Multiplication","Copy an element already existing in a product, but change it in a counterintuitive way","1 List components. 2 Make a copy of one of the components and change it. 3 Look for potential benefits.","Procter & Gamble Febreze Plug: worksheet with columns component, attributes, virtual product"],["(Task) Unification","Assign additional tasks to an existing resource","1 List the product's internal and external components. 2 Select a component and assign an additional task. 3 Look for potential benefits. 4 Modify the concept to improve.","(1) internal takes the job of another internal component: coffee-maker jug with measuring marks; (2) internal takes the job of an external component: Nissan easy-fill tyre alert; (3) external takes the job of an internal component: billboard becomes the point of sale, commuters scan products and the shopping list is sent to the grocery store"],["Attribute dependency","One thing changes as another thing changes (create a dependency between attributes)","List attributes (external and internal); fill in \"as attribute __ changes, so does attribute __\"; identify potential benefits; modify and adapt the concept.","Alarm clock: as elapsed time of ringing increases, loudness increases"]]},{"type":"note","text":"Slide pictures for division (water skiing) and attribute dependency (sunglasses, toasting glasses) are not captioned; the functional/physical/preservational split and the picture readings above beyond the cake and dashboard are standard SIT teaching, not slide text. Learn the definitions and the procedures."},"**Attribute dependency, internal versus external attributes**: external = attributes of the surrounding environment and people related to the topic (width, length, height, gender, age, preferences, time); internal = attributes of the product itself (size, shape, colour).","**Alarm clock worked example (slide)**: external attributes = time of day, elapsed time of alarm ringing, duration of sleep, characteristics of user, restfulness of sleep; internal attributes = volume of alarm, type of alarm sound, colour, size, shape. A dependency: as elapsed time increases, the loudness of the alarm increases (or: the sound depends on how restful the sleep was)."]},{"heading":"SCAMPER: question prompts and extra examples from the slides","items":["Slide framing of each letter: **Substitute** = what can be replaced (components, materials, people)? **Combine** = what can be combined (other features, devices)? **Adapt** = what can be added (new elements or functions)? **Modify / magnify / minify** = change size, shape, colour or another attribute. **Put to other use** = other industry or purpose? **Eliminate or minimise** = what can be removed or simplified? **Reverse, reengineer or rearrange** = what can be swapped or flipped?","Prompt questions: Substitute: can we use another alternative of X, replace the product with a simpler one, where else could we sell it? Combine: can we merge two products, use two at the same time, mix components, combine X and Y technologies? Adapt: what would we change to reach better results, what are the tradeoffs, how can we make the product more flexible (you must also define how you measure 'better')? Modify: what if we had more or less of a feature, what if bigger or smaller, can it be portable? Put to another use: what other markets, users or companies might use it, can end-of-life products be recycled or repurposed? Eliminate: what would happen if we removed this part, can we get the same output without it, what if we had half the resources? Reverse: what if we reverse the process, rearrange the order of service or use, consider it backwards, interchange elements?","Extra lecture examples: windows for a children's playhouse (glass to plastic for safety); PLA biodegradable plastic bottles; 'sushi pizza'; phone plus camera; cars with built-in wifi; smartphone with interchangeable lenses; sunglasses for babies; foldable extending table; coffee filters used as mask filters in COVID-19; drones repurposed for wind-turbine inspection; removing cigarette lighters from cars; app with unnecessary buttons eliminated; fast food 'pay then eat'; crowdfunding platform where investors pitch to backers.","**Fast-food chain example (Adapt, Eliminate, Reverse)**: Adapt = selling restaurants and real estate instead of just hamburgers; Eliminate = letting customers serve themselves, avoiding expensive waiters; Reverse = customers pay before they eat.","**Redesign a common product** exercise on the slides: apply all seven SCAMPER letters to a desk lamp, toaster, washing machine, car key fob, home camera, sleep mask, extension lead or safety shoe."]},{"heading":"IDEO approach (summary)","items":["**Understand** the market, the client, the technology.","**Observe** what confuses, what is hated, what is not satisfied.","**Visualise/realise**: role-play, storyboard, early prototype.","**Evaluate and refine**: several prototypes, concurrent engineering.","**Implement**: verify the final product works, commercialise, market."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Apply SCAMPER (all seven letters) to a named product.","Name the five SIT tools and give an example of two.","Compare SCAMPER with the 5-step creative process (prompting tool vs full process)."]}],"pitfalls":["Applying only some SCAMPER letters when asked for all seven.","Confusing SIT subtraction with SCAMPER eliminate (similar, but SIT then asks how the benefit arises) and attribute dependency with modify.","Stopping at ideas without selection criteria."]},{"id":"types-of-innovation","title":"Types of innovation","priority":18,"tier":"possible","freq":"est. medium","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**Know the types with a lecture example each: product, process, service, business model, incremental/sustaining, frugal, reinnovation, disruptive.**","mnemonic":"P-P-S-B + I-F-R-D: Product, Process, Service, Business model; Incremental, Frugal, Re-innovation, Disruptive","source":["CT4107_Notes_01_Introduction","CT4107_Notes_02a_Week2_Part1"],"sections":[{"heading":"Types with definition and example","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Type","Definition","Lecture example"],"rows":[["Product innovation","New or improved product: features, performance, look; often built on a core technology","Peloton: integrated hardware, software, social fitness platform; better camera on a phone"],["Process innovation","New or significantly improved methods of producing a good or delivering a service: efficiency, effectiveness, value creation; remove bottlenecks, cut cost, increase speed and quality","Agile transformed how teams build software; automation; improving canteen, hospital, transport"],["Service innovation","Improving the customer experience","Loyalty programme, easier online booking, personalised support"],["Business model innovation","New ways to make money or deliver value","Switch to subscription, new market, partnering"],["Incremental / sustaining innovation","Series of small improvements to an existing product/service to add or sustain value; lower risk than a brand-new product; targets existing customers","Apple/Samsung adding camera, processor, sensors; MS Office adding cloud and collaboration; cars with lane and parking assist; PS5 with backward compatibility"],["Frugal innovation","Reducing complexity and cost of a good and its production, for overlooked consumers; volume offsets thin margins; remove non-essential features; may need more durability and unconventional distribution","GE portable ECG for rural India: lightweight, battery-operated, a fraction of hospital unit cost"],["Reinnovation","Reusing or repurposing existing materials/products in new value-adding ways","Patagonia and Adidas melt ocean plastics and bottles into performance polyester yarn"],["Disruptive innovation","Enters a niche at the low-price/commercial end, then rapidly overtakes incumbents through flexibility, pricing, novelty and a fast-growing customer base","Airbnb vs hotels; 3D printing vs bespoke tool making; PCs vs mainframes; digital photography vs film; budget airlines"]]}]},{"heading":"Sustaining vs disruptive","items":["**Sustaining/incremental**: improves existing products for existing customers; strengthens incumbents.","**Disruptive**: starts simpler/cheaper for new or low-end customers, improves, and displaces incumbents.","Why disruptors win: incumbents ignore low margins, are tied to existing customers, and are slow."]},{"heading":"Extra from the slides","items":["The lecture's diagram of innovation types shows seven circles around 'Innovation': **product, process, incremental, sustaining, frugal, reinnovation, disruptive**. The second deck then lists four **types of incremental innovation**: product, process, service and business model.","Incremental innovation (slide definition): a series of small-scale improvements to an existing product or service to add or sustain value; rather than a risky brand-new product it modifies a current one to improve retention, relevance and longevity. Sustaining innovation is 'pretty much the same': improving an existing product, service or process, often for existing customers, to strengthen market position (Apple/Samsung features, MS Office cloud integration, cars with lane/parking assist).","Process innovation outcomes (slide diagram): efficiency and cost reduction, quality improvement, speed and time savings, flexibility and adaptability, innovation enablement, competitive advantage. Method: scrutinise workflows, redesign procedures, use technology such as automation to remove bottlenecks. Exercise: look at services you use (canteen, hospital, transport) and ask how they can be improved.","Examples on the first deck: noise-cancelling earphones and continuous process improvement (incremental); PlayStation 5 backward compatibility (sustaining); Peloton (product); Agile (process); 3D printing disrupting bespoke tool making (disruptive).","Frugal innovation: designing for 'overlooked consumers' may also call for more durability and unconventional distribution channels when selling."]},{"heading":"Typical exam angles","items":["Define and distinguish four types of innovation with examples.","Is idea X incremental or disruptive? Justify.","Explain frugal innovation and when it fits."]}],"pitfalls":["Giving the type name but no example.","Calling any new feature \"disruptive\": disruption means displacing incumbents from below.","Confusing process innovation with product innovation."]},{"id":"pitch-video-deliverables","title":"Video pitch and Student Entrepreneur Awards","priority":19,"tier":"low","freq":"est. low","marks":"2-5 marks","summary":"**The final deliverable is a 2-minute video pitch: be clear and upfront about the idea, USP, competition and facts and figures with references; sound quality is vital.**","mnemonic":"IDEA, USP, COMPETITION, FACTS + REFS; sound is VITAL","source":["CT4107_Notes_11_Video","CT4107_Notes_11b_Useful_Tools_Links","CT4107_Notes_09_Business_Model_Canvas"],"sections":[{"heading":"Video pitch rules","items":["Be very clear and upfront about: your idea, USP, competition, facts and figures on need and market.","Cite sources for facts (e.g. author and year); show the full references as an image at the end.","Use the space well and really sell your idea; **sound quality is vital**.","Include facts, figures and references."]},{"heading":"Student Entrepreneur Awards (Enterprise Ireland)","items":["Stage 1: submit a 2-minute video pitch (provisional close 6 March 2026).","Stage 2: top 50 submit a business plan (announced 27 March, plans due 1 May).","Stage 3: top 10 chosen (15 May) and present to a judging panel (28 May); winner announced 29 May.","Note: dates are provisional on the slide; the course deadline for the video is week 12."]},{"heading":"Production tools (not examinable detail)","items":["Free clips and images (Pixabay, Pexels, Unsplash), royalty-free music, text-to-audio, online editors (Photopea, Kapwing, Animoto), desktop editors (Filmora leaves a watermark, VideoPad does not)."]},{"heading":"Enterprise Ireland guidance shown on the slides","items":["How to sell the idea to the judges: be **passionate** (judges want the people as well as the idea); be **impactful and clear** (two minutes to tell the full story, define the proposition clearly in the first **30 seconds**); stage one is the two-minute video and it is referenced through every later stage.","Five video requirements: (1) clearly define the business proposition/idea, (2) explain the problem it solves and how it works, (3) highlight how the idea is innovative, (4) identify the target customer and why they would buy it, (5) highlight the idea's **export potential**.","How to stand out: imagine pitching to customers (level tone of delivery), practice makes perfect, record in a quiet place (no background noise, good sound), ask friends or family for feedback.","Entry criteria: students own, control and manage their IP where applicable; not-for-profit and social entrepreneurship applicants must operate in a commercial domain; ambition to start the business in Ireland with headquarters here; **maximum five students per team** (the course teams are 2-4).","What judges look for: is there a market/commercial potential? what is the unique selling point? has the team built a proof of concept? have students tested with real users in the target market? does the team understand key financials?","Stages: stage 1 two-minute video; stage 2 top 50 submit a business plan; stage 3 ten projects present to the judging panel at the ceremony. The slides also show the 2025 dates (video 14 March, top 50 on 4 April, plans 9 May, top 10 on 23 May, final 5-6 June); the 2026 dates in the other deck are provisional.","Course note: one deliverable left after the canvas, the video, to be completed by the end of week 12."]}],"pitfalls":["Pitch with claims but no sources.","Poor audio.","Describing the product instead of the benefit and differentiator."]}],"flashcards":[{"id":"bh-def","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"What is a beachhead market?","back":"The single specific segment you enter first and aim to dominate, then use as a base to attack adjacent markets.","hook":"D-Day landing"},{"id":"bh-why1","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Why target only one market first?","back":"Focus scarce resources, tailored message, word of mouth stays in one community, easier to dominate."},{"id":"bh-3cond","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Three conditions that define a market?","back":"Buys the same product; similar sales process (persona, value prop); word of mouth exists.","hook":"Product, Process, Word of mouth"},{"id":"bh-crit","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Name the 7 beachhead selection criteria.","back":"Economically attractive, accessible to sales force, strong value proposition, complete product, competition, strategic value, personal alignment.","hook":"E-A-V-C-C-S-P"},{"id":"bh-scale","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Rating scale on the beachhead worksheet?","back":"Very High (best), High, Medium, Low, Show Stopper (worst)."},{"id":"bh-show","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"What does a show stopper do?","back":"Eliminates that candidate market regardless of other scores."},{"id":"bh-n","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"How many candidates do you narrow to before choosing?","back":"Top 5-6 candidate markets."},{"id":"bh-rank","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"How are candidates ranked on the worksheet?","back":"1 = most attractive; plus record the key deciding factors."},{"id":"bh-acc","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Criterion 2 \"accessible to our sales force\" asks?","back":"Can we reach decision makers cheaply through channels, contacts, referrals?"},{"id":"bh-strat","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Criterion 6 \"strategic value\" asks?","back":"Does winning this segment open adjacent markets (references, assets, brand)?"},{"id":"bh-comp","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Criterion 5 \"competition\": what counts as a competitor?","back":"Incumbents and alternatives including the do-nothing option."},{"id":"bh-comp2","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Criterion 4 \"complete product\" asks?","back":"Can we deliver the whole solution, including partners and complementary assets, soon?"},{"id":"bh-trap","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Biggest-segment trap?","back":"Largest market is not best; pick the one you can win and reach first."},{"id":"seg-def","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Define market segmentation.","back":"Dividing customers into groups similar in ways such as age, gender, interests, spending habits, task and need."},{"id":"seg-step","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Which DE step is market segmentation?","back":"Step 1 (market evaluation), then Step 2 select a beachhead."},{"id":"seg-end","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"End user vs economic buyer?","back":"End user actually uses the product; economic buyer pays. End user must be a real person, not an organisation."},{"id":"seg-task","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Segmentation matrix field \"Task\"?","back":"What the end user does that you significantly affect or enable for the first time."},{"id":"seg-urg","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Segmentation matrix \"Urgency of need\"?","back":"How urgent the problem or opportunity is for the end user."},{"id":"seg-lead","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"What is a lead (lighthouse) customer?","back":"An influential customer who, if they buy, others notice and follow."},{"id":"seg-conc","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"\"Concentration of buyers\" options?","back":"Monopoly, oligopoly (few buyers) or many competitive buyers."},{"id":"seg-size","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"How precisely is market size estimated in the matrix?","back":"Order of magnitude: 10s, 100s, 1Ks, 10Ks, 100Ks, 1M end users."},{"id":"seg-full","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"\"Other components for a full solution\"?","back":"Complementary assets you lack but must build or acquire to give a total solution."},{"id":"seg-journey","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Why view the customer journey end to end?","back":"Customer sees whole experience, not only your touchpoints (McKinsey)."},{"id":"seg-bases","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Four segmentation bases?","back":"Demographic, geographic, psychographic, behavioural."},{"id":"seg-cake","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Urban Cake Shop segments?","back":"Morning commuters, seasonal celebrators, one-time weddings, casual drop-ins."},{"id":"seg-gym","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Weightlifting-rack segments in lecture?","back":"Ordinary, high-end, university and home gyms, rehab, apartments/hotels, Olympic athletes."},{"id":"tam-def","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Define TAM (Aulet Step 4).","back":"Annual revenue per year you would earn at 100% share of the beachhead market."},{"id":"tam-sam","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Define SAM.","back":"Part of TAM you can realistically target and serve given geography, pricing, distribution."},{"id":"tam-som","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Define SOM.","back":"Part of SAM you can realistically capture given your sales ability, competition, saturation."},{"id":"tam-order","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Order of TAM, SAM, SOM?","back":"TAM >= SAM >= SOM (funnel).","hook":"Total, Serve, Snatch"},{"id":"tam-bu","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Bottom-up TAM formula?","back":"N users x units per purchase x purchases per year x price."},{"id":"tam-td","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Top-down TAM method?","back":"Start from a big industry/population figure and cut by percentages."},{"id":"tam-cmp","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Top-down vs bottom-up strength?","back":"Top-down is a quick sanity check; bottom-up is credible and tied to your price and customers."},{"id":"tam-crit","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"What does bottom-up need to be effective?","back":"Realistic data and realistic estimation."},{"id":"tam-one","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Annualised one-time-charge revenue?","back":"(Price x units per user) / average life in years."},{"id":"tam-bud","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Budget method per-user revenue?","back":"Total budget per user x % that could go to this solution."},{"id":"tam-comp","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Third per-user revenue method?","back":"Comparable products converted to annual revenue."},{"id":"tam-3","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Three ways to estimate annual revenue per user?","back":"One-time charge annualised, budget available, comparables."},{"id":"tam-preg","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Irish pregnancy-app funnel?","back":"2,465,802 females, about 75,713 pregnancies a year, about 30,306 first-time mothers."},{"id":"tam-perch","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Perch gym-rack units?","back":"3,000 high-end gyms x 4 racks = 12,000 units (35,000 gyms in USA)."},{"id":"tam-q","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"What question does TAM answer?","back":"Is the beachhead market big enough to be interesting?"},{"id":"qvp-def","topicId":"qvp","front":"Define Quantified Value Proposition.","back":"Converts the persona's benefits into a tangible metric on their top priority."},{"id":"qvp-xy","topicId":"qvp","front":"QVP as-is vs to-be?","back":"As-Is state X vs Possible state Y on priority #1; value = difference."},{"id":"qvp-dim","topicId":"qvp","front":"On what dimension is value measured?","back":"The persona's #1 priority."},{"id":"qvp-why","topicId":"qvp","front":"Why quantify?","back":"If you cannot measure it you cannot demonstrate added value."},{"id":"qvp-check","topicId":"qvp","front":"What must you check about other priorities?","back":"That none decrease and offset the benefit."},{"id":"qvp-focus","topicId":"qvp","front":"QVP focus: features or gains?","back":"What customers gain, not technology, features or functions."},{"id":"qvp-val","topicId":"qvp","front":"How should a QVP be validated?","back":"With multiple target customers, supported by evidence."},{"id":"qvp-meater","topicId":"qvp","front":"Meater example numbers?","back":"Loss USD 63k to 40k: saving 23k; USD 7 per cow, reusable four times."},{"id":"qvp-sun","topicId":"qvp","front":"X-treme sunscreen QVP claims?","back":"Lasts 6x longer, 5x more effective, 3x less quantity; 100% effective after all events."},{"id":"qvp-uspvs","topicId":"qvp","front":"Value proposition vs QVP vs USP?","back":"Promise of benefit; same promise with numbers; what makes you different and hard to copy."},{"id":"qvp-pg","topicId":"qvp","front":"Pains and gains?","back":"Pains = obstacles and frustrations; gains = what the persona wants and how they measure success."},{"id":"qvp-acc","topicId":"qvp","front":"Student accommodation app pains?","back":"Search too long and stressful, landlords slow to reply, no unified search app."},{"id":"pro-def","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Define customer / ideal customer profile.","back":"Characteristics of the people most likely to buy and get high value from your product."},{"id":"pro-6","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Six parts of the End-User Profile?","back":"Demographics, psychographics, proxy products, watering holes, day in the life, priorities.","hook":"D-P-P-W-D-P"},{"id":"pro-proxy","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Proxy products?","back":"Other products the end user owns and values, correlated with your target."},{"id":"pro-water","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Watering holes?","back":"Places, associations, online platforms where they gather, ranked by priority and intensity."},{"id":"pro-pri","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Priorities rule in the profile?","back":"List priorities with weights that add to 100."},{"id":"pro-why","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Why build a profile (4 reasons)?","back":"Segment by behaviour, keep focus on end user, deepen understanding, calculate TAM."},{"id":"pro-vs","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Profile vs persona?","back":"Profile is the average group; persona is one specific representative fictional person."},{"id":"per-def","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Define persona.","back":"Fictional but realistic person in the beachhead who best exemplifies the End-User Profile."},{"id":"per-why","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Why use a persona?","back":"Focus the organisation; touchstone for all decisions."},{"id":"per-demo","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Demographics vs psychographics?","back":"Demographics: external, quantitative (age, income). Psychographics: values, goals, lifestyle, qualitative."},{"id":"per-fields","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Persona fields?","back":"Picture, background, age, gender, behaviours, personality, goals, needs, motivations, pain points, spending habits."},{"id":"per-pain","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Pain prompts?","back":"Bad day? Afraid of? Keeps her awake? Responsible for? Obstacles?"},{"id":"per-gain","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Gain prompts?","back":"What does she want? How does she measure success? How could she benefit? What can we offer?"},{"id":"per-char","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Charlotte persona?","back":"The Scared Cyclist: main persona of the London cycling example."},{"id":"per-step","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Which DE step is the persona?","back":"Step 5."},{"id":"lc-def","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Full life-cycle use case also called?","back":"The Customer Journey (Step 6)."},{"id":"lc-why","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Why do a life-cycle use case?","back":"Shows how product fits the persona's workflow and exposes barriers to adoption."},{"id":"lc-10","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Name the ten stages.","back":"Need, find, analyse, acquire, pay, install, use, determine value, buy more, tell others.","hook":"NEED-FIND-WEIGH-GET-PAY-SET UP-USE-VALUE-REBUY-REFER"},{"id":"lc-q","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Four detail questions per stage?","back":"Who is involved, when, where, how."},{"id":"lc-best","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"\"Best product doesn't always win\" means?","back":"The product the customer is aware of may win."},{"id":"lc-1","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Stage 1 informs which steps?","back":"Developing solution and value proposition."},{"id":"lc-2","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Stage 2 (find) informs?","back":"Go-to-market strategy and cost of customer acquisition."},{"id":"lc-9","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Stage 9 (buy more) informs?","back":"Go-to-market, business model, pricing, expansion plan, customer lifetime value."},{"id":"lc-10b","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Stage 10 (tell others) informs?","back":"Go-to-market strategy and incentives (word of mouth)."},{"id":"lc-3","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Stage 3 (analyse) informs?","back":"MVBP, value proposition, competitive positioning."},{"id":"pmr-def","topicId":"primary-market-research","front":"PMR rules of thumb?","back":"Starts now, never ends, underlies almost every step."},{"id":"pmr-list","topicId":"primary-market-research","front":"Name PMR methods.","back":"Interviews, observation, immersion, secondary research, user tests, focus groups, diary studies, landing pages."},{"id":"pmr-ctx","topicId":"primary-market-research","front":"Contextual interview?","back":"Interview where the product will be used; open questions, let subject lead."},{"id":"pmr-imm","topicId":"primary-market-research","front":"Immersion?","back":"Become the user (e.g. team uses 4 CAD programs)."},{"id":"pmr-diary","topicId":"primary-market-research","front":"Longitudinal diary study?","back":"Users record experiences over days or weeks."},{"id":"pmr-land","topicId":"primary-market-research","front":"Landing page test?","back":"Page with call to action (email or card capture) to test demand for a not-yet-built product."},{"id":"pmr-iter","topicId":"primary-market-research","front":"PMR success = iteration of?","back":"Engaging people (people skills) plus common-sense data analysis."},{"id":"pmr-show","topicId":"primary-market-research","front":"When to show the product in an interview?","back":"Last, only if time is left and they ask."},{"id":"bmc-def","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"What is the Business Model Canvas?","back":"One-page template of nine blocks showing how a business creates, delivers and captures value."},{"id":"bmc-9","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Name the nine BMC blocks.","back":"Customer segments, value propositions, channels, customer relationships, revenue streams, key resources, key activities, key partnerships, cost structure.","hook":"WHO, WHAT, HOW, MONEY"},{"id":"bmc-4","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Four areas the nine blocks cover?","back":"Customers, product/service, infrastructure/operations, financial viability."},{"id":"bmc-cs","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Customer segments block asks?","back":"Who do you sell to and help? Which groups, with what personas?"},{"id":"bmc-vp","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Value propositions block asks?","back":"What problems do you solve and why do customers prefer you?"},{"id":"bmc-ch","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Channels block asks?","back":"How you communicate, sell, deliver and service; the journey from awareness to sale."},{"id":"bmc-cr","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Customer relationships block asks?","back":"How you interact: acquire, retain, upsell; face-to-face or remote."},{"id":"bmc-rs","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Revenue streams block asks?","back":"What income customers generate, pricing strategy, mix across segments."},{"id":"bmc-kr","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Key resources kinds?","back":"Physical, intellectual property, human, financial."},{"id":"bmc-ka","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Key activities block?","back":"Tasks needed to deliver value: R&D, production, marketing, sales, service, admin."},{"id":"bmc-kp","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Key partnerships block?","back":"Suppliers and collaborators critical to the business, what they provide."},{"id":"bmc-co","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Cost structure block asks?","back":"Cost to launch and run; biggest cost; fixed vs variable; economies of scale."},{"id":"bmc-why","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Advantage of canvas over full plan?","back":"Get customer feedback sooner and adjust as the model evolves."},{"id":"bmc-soc","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Can the BMC suit social enterprises?","back":"Yes: customers can be read as clients, beneficiaries or audiences."},{"id":"bmc-grp","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Why is Groupon two-sided?","back":"Consumers want discounts; merchants want advertising and a sell-and-fulfil platform; revenue is a percentage fee."},{"id":"bmc-rev","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Groupon revenue stream?","back":"Percentage fee on transactions, plus ancillary new products."},{"id":"bmb-model","topicId":"business-model-basics","front":"Business model definition?","back":"How a company creates, delivers and captures value (who, what, when, where, why)."},{"id":"bmb-plan","topicId":"business-model-basics","front":"Business plan definition?","back":"Detailed document of strategy and expected financial performance over several years."},{"id":"bmb-rev","topicId":"business-model-basics","front":"Revenue model?","back":"How the business makes money, including pricing and sales channels (ads, subscriptions)."},{"id":"bmb-goog","topicId":"business-model-basics","front":"Google's business model innovation?","back":"Simple text ads targeted by search keyword, giving advertisers better effectiveness data."},{"id":"bmb-vs","topicId":"business-model-basics","front":"Why model before plan?","back":"Customer feedback sooner and easier adjustment; plan then more realistic."},{"id":"spec-def","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"High-level product specification?","back":"Visual representation of the product showing benefits that address the beachhead's main pain points."},{"id":"spec-rules","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"Rules for the product spec?","back":"Do not build yet, customer view, benefits not features, visual, no price, living document."},{"id":"spec-price","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"Should the spec mention price?","back":"No: it is far too early."},{"id":"spec-bro","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"Brochure benefit structure?","back":"One primary benefit on persona #1 priority, two additional benefits, magnitude, call to action."},{"id":"spec-mag","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"How to present magnitude claims?","back":"As pilot targets to validate, not guarantees."},{"id":"mvbp-def","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"MVBP three conditions?","back":"Customer gets value, customer pays, product starts the feedback loop.","hook":"Value, Pay, Feedback"},{"id":"mvbp-step","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"Which DE step is MVBP?","back":"Step 22 (spec is step 7, QVP step 8)."},{"id":"mvbp-vs","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"MVBP vs MVP?","back":"MVBP must be a viable business: customers actually pay."},{"id":"core-def","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Define core (Step 10).","back":"What you do better than anyone, invest in and protect; basis of lasting USP."},{"id":"core-moat","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Three dimensions of a good moat?","back":"Unique, Grows, Important (tied to the QVP).","hook":"U-G-I"},{"id":"core-forms","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Forms a core can take?","back":"Algorithm/knowledge, process, expertise."},{"id":"core-moatdef","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Competitive moat origin?","back":"Warren Buffett's economic moat: advantages protecting long-term profits and share."},{"id":"core-ikea","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"IKEA core?","back":"Logistics and vendor management (value: low cost, good design)."},{"id":"core-dhl","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"DHL core?","back":"Operational excellence (fast reliable delivery)."},{"id":"core-apple","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Apple core?","back":"User experience: consistently delivering quality experiences."},{"id":"core-amz","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Amazon core?","back":"Huge experience in e-commerce services."},{"id":"core-goog","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Google core?","back":"Evolved from search to knowing everything (data)."},{"id":"pos-chart","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Competitive position chart axes?","back":"Persona's top two priorities; good state far from origin."},{"id":"pos-dn","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"What must be plotted besides competitors?","back":"The customer's do-nothing / status quo option."},{"id":"pos-goal","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Best place on the chart?","back":"Top right: good on both priorities."},{"id":"pos-swot","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"SWOT stands for?","back":"Strengths, Weaknesses (internal); Opportunities, Threats (external)."},{"id":"pos-sun","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"SunSpring chart axes?","back":"Efficiency (x) and reliability (y) vs with-battery and no-battery alternatives."},{"id":"woo-def","topicId":"windows-of-opportunity","front":"Window of opportunity?","back":"A time when the buyer or influencer is particularly open to considering your offer."},{"id":"woo-trig","topicId":"windows-of-opportunity","front":"Trigger?","back":"A specific action inside the window creating urgency to act."},{"id":"woo-ex","topicId":"windows-of-opportunity","front":"Examples of triggers?","back":"Discount, limited supply, time-limited offer, free stuff, sales-rep interaction."},{"id":"woo-win","topicId":"windows-of-opportunity","front":"Examples of windows?","back":"End of fiscal year, holiday season, life transition, change of leadership, crisis, security breach."},{"id":"de-who","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Six DE questions?","back":"Who is your customer, what can you do for them, how do they acquire, how do you make money, how do you design and build, how do you scale."},{"id":"de-24","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"How many steps in Disciplined Entrepreneurship?","back":"24 steps (Bill Aulet, MIT)."},{"id":"de-1to5","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Steps in theme \"Who is your customer?\"","back":"1 Market segmentation, 2 beachhead market, 3 end-user profile, 4 TAM, 5 persona, 9 identify next 10 customers."},{"id":"de-6to11","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Steps in theme \"What can you do for your customer?\"","back":"6 Full life-cycle use case, 7 high-level product spec, 8 quantify value proposition, 10 define core, 11 chart competitive position."},{"id":"de-12","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Steps in theme \"How does your customer acquire your product?\"","back":"12 Decision-making unit (DMU), 13 map the process to acquire a paying customer, 18 map the sales process."},{"id":"de-15","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Steps in theme \"How do you make money?\"","back":"15 Business model, 16 pricing framework, 17 lifetime value (LTV), 19 cost of customer acquisition (COCA)."},{"id":"de-20","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Steps in themes \"design and build\" and \"scale\"?","back":"Build: 20 key assumptions, 21 test them, 22 MVBP, 23 dogs eat the dog food. Scale: 14 follow-on TAM, 24 product plan."},{"id":"de-ai","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Treat AI tool output as?","back":"Unproven hypotheses; keep AI interaction log and delta log."},{"id":"de-team","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Team size?","back":"Minimum 2, maximum 4."},{"id":"de-pitch","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Elevator pitch components?","back":"Benefit, differentiator, ask."},{"id":"de-deliv","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Deliverable sequence?","back":"Idea, segmentation/beachhead/profile, persona/use case, QVP/spec, BMC, video pitch."},{"id":"vid-len","topicId":"pitch-video-deliverables","front":"Video pitch length?","back":"2 minutes."},{"id":"vid-must","topicId":"pitch-video-deliverables","front":"Key video requirements?","back":"Clear idea, USP, competition, facts with references, vital sound quality."},{"id":"vid-stage","topicId":"pitch-video-deliverables","front":"Student Entrepreneur Awards stages?","back":"Video, top 50 submit business plan, top 10 present to judges."},{"id":"inn-eq","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Innovation equation?","back":"Innovation = Invention x Commercialisation."},{"id":"inn-def","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Invention vs innovation?","back":"Invention is a creative idea; innovation makes it feasible and turns it into something customers value."},{"id":"inn-ent","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Define creation of new business.","back":"Forming a new venture producing an offering with value that is economically sustainable."},{"id":"inn-proc","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Entrepreneurial process steps?","back":"Identify opportunity, establish vision, persuade others, gather resources, create venture, adapt over time."},{"id":"inn-3m","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"3M sticky note origin?","back":"1968 Spencer Silver sought a strong adhesive and accidentally made a low-tack reusable one."},{"id":"inn-scotch","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Scotch Tape to Sellotape shows?","back":"Innovation builds on old ideas: 1930 packaging tape resold as an office commodity."},{"id":"inn-teach","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Can entrepreneurship be taught?","back":"Yes (Aulet): it is a process, not magic."},{"id":"inn-core","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Core technology example?","back":"Internal combustion engine: cars, planes, boats; then fuel injection, turbocharging."},{"id":"inn-3","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Three things in the class?","back":"Idea, team, process."},{"id":"typ-prod","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Product innovation example?","back":"Peloton integrated hardware, software and social fitness platform."},{"id":"typ-proc","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Process innovation?","back":"New or improved methods of producing or delivering: efficiency, cost, speed, quality (e.g. Agile)."},{"id":"typ-inc","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Incremental innovation?","back":"Small improvements to an existing product, e.g. phone camera upgrades."},{"id":"typ-frug","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Frugal innovation?","back":"Reduce complexity and cost for overlooked consumers, e.g. GE portable ECG for rural India."},{"id":"typ-re","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Reinnovation?","back":"Reusing materials in new value, e.g. Patagonia and Adidas ocean-plastic yarn."},{"id":"typ-dis","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Disruptive innovation?","back":"Low-end niche entrant that overtakes incumbents: PCs, digital photography, budget airlines, Airbnb."},{"id":"typ-svc","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Service innovation?","back":"Improves customer experience: loyalty programme, easier booking, personalised support."},{"id":"typ-bm","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Business model innovation?","back":"New ways to make money or deliver value: subscription, new market, partnership."},{"id":"cre-4","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Four elements of creativity?","back":"Problem finding and solving, relevance, value, novelty."},{"id":"cre-5","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"The 5-step creative process?","back":"Objective, data gathering, problem design, ideation, selection.","hook":"OBJECTIVE-DATA-PROBLEM-IDEAS-PICK"},{"id":"cre-3","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Three ways to define the problem?","back":"Rephrase, expose and challenge assumptions, find multiple perspectives."},{"id":"cre-tags","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Data tags in step 2?","back":"Vital, interesting, insight."},{"id":"cre-ladder","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Ladder of abstraction in problem design?","back":"Problem too broad gives too many solutions; too specific only scratches the surface."},{"id":"cre-ein","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Einstein 55/5 rule?","back":"Spend 55 of 60 minutes defining the problem."},{"id":"cre-tools","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Problem analysis tools?","back":"5 Whys, root cause analysis, fishbone diagram."},{"id":"cre-goal","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Goal vs solution?","back":"A solution is an answer; a goal is a desired state of being."},{"id":"cre-ideas","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Step 4 produces?","back":"Possibilities, not solutions; selection (step 5) picks solutions."},{"id":"cre-good","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Characteristics of a good business idea?","back":"Solves a problem, market need, sizeable segment, attractive, achievable, durable, differentiated, scalable."},{"id":"sc-7","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SCAMPER letters?","back":"Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Modify, Put to another use, Eliminate, Reverse.","hook":"S-C-A-M-P-E-R"},{"id":"sc-ex","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SCAMPER example for Reverse?","back":"Fast food: pay then eat."},{"id":"sit-5","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Five SIT tools?","back":"Subtraction, division, multiplication, unification, attribute dependency."},{"id":"sit-idea","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SIT principle?","back":"Start from the solution and work backwards to the problem."},{"id":"sit-uni","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SIT unification example?","back":"Billboards become point of sale: scan products, shopping list goes to the store."},{"id":"sit-attr","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Component vs attribute?","back":"Component is a tangible part; attribute is a characteristic of product or environment (e.g. alarm volume)."},{"id":"ideo","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"IDEO steps?","back":"Understand, observe, visualise/realise, evaluate/refine, implement."},{"id":"x-ryan","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Ryanair beachhead (lecture quiz)?","back":"Fliers interested in low cost, with trimmings removed."},{"id":"x-rabbit","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"Proverb for \"why only one market\"?","back":"A person who chases two rabbits catches neither."},{"id":"x-narrow","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Stage 1B narrowing form: six questions?","back":"Accessible to sales force? Well-funded? Compelling reason to buy? Whole product deliverable? Entrenched competition? Can you leverage it to enter others?"},{"id":"x-brain","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Stage 1 brainstorm form columns?","back":"Industry or similar category, end users, what they would use it for."},{"id":"x-holy","topicId":"beachhead-market","front":"\"Holy grail of specificity\" path?","back":"Brainstorm, narrow to a few segments, pick beachhead, segment it further, target profile, TAM, one persona."},{"id":"x-custview","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"\"Very important step\" slide message?","back":"See the world through the customer's eyes, not the company's perspective."},{"id":"x-pregseg","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"Pregnancy app segments chosen from?","back":"Pregnant women, first-time mothers (chosen), fathers-to-be, family, close friends, doctors."},{"id":"x-mck","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"McKinsey six journey actions (gist)?","back":"Identify journeys from customer view, understand touchpoint navigation, anticipate needs, see what works, prioritise gaps, fix root causes."},{"id":"x-perchpro","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"High-end chain gyms: pros for smart racks?","back":"Less price-sensitive, image-conscious, easy to expand to other sites and follow-on markets, large market."},{"id":"x-perchcon","topicId":"market-segmentation","front":"High-end chain gyms: cons?","back":"No contacts yet; equipment must be top quality; athlete improvement is not their main motivation."},{"id":"x-sanity","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Role of the beachhead TAM slide?","back":"A sanity check you are headed the right way: not too small (uninteresting), not too big (danger)."},{"id":"x-sweet","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"TAM sweet spot?","back":"Big enough to give positive cash flow, small enough to dominate."},{"id":"x-venn","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Total / Served available / Target market?","back":"Everyone; those reachable by your sales channel; the startup's initial buyers."},{"id":"x-rent","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Rental units example, three levels?","back":"42.58M US units (USD 1.02B), 0.59M Manhattan (USD 14.2M), 190K high-rise (USD 1.4M); 2 residents/unit, 30% adoption, USD 1 per month."},{"id":"x-rent2","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"USD 1.02B rental TAM: how built?","back":"42.58M units x 2 residents x USD 1 x 12 months."},{"id":"x-prefbu","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Which TAM approach does the lecture prefer?","back":"Bottom-up from primary research; top-down misses subtleties and is less compelling."},{"id":"x-tdws","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Top-down worksheet structure?","back":"Largest demographic then 1st, 2nd, 3rd segmentation by profile (each with % of previous, assumptions, sources) to end users in beachhead."},{"id":"x-gym","topicId":"tam-sam-som","front":"Smart-rack funnel numbers?","back":"35,000 US gyms, 3,000 high-end chains, 4 racks each = 12k+ units."},{"id":"x-vp4","topicId":"qvp","front":"Four essential elements of a value proposition?","back":"Customer's perspective, what's in it for them, why choose you (differentiation), prove it (quantification).","hook":"Need, Gain, Why you, Proof"},{"id":"x-kelvin","topicId":"qvp","front":"Lord Kelvin slide point?","back":"If you cannot measure and express it in numbers, you do not really know it: benefit becomes a tangible metric."},{"id":"x-gcpr","topicId":"qvp","front":"Gain creators and pain relievers?","back":"Products and services relieve the persona's pains and create the gains; list up to 5 each and link to the persona."},{"id":"x-collab","topicId":"qvp","front":"Collaboration tool QVP numbers?","back":"Ideation 3 weeks to 2 (33%), design 10 days to 5 (50%), overall 18% faster to launch."},{"id":"x-brd","topicId":"qvp","front":"Meater BRD tree (200 cattle)?","back":"As-Is 50 deaths + 150 ill = USD 63k; with sensor 25 deaths + 175 ill = USD 40k; saving USD 23k."},{"id":"x-brdunit","topicId":"qvp","front":"Meater unit costs?","back":"Death 997 per head; each illness 89 (treatment 16, feed efficiency 15, quality grade 58)."},{"id":"x-sunscr","topicId":"qvp","front":"Marathon sunscreen baseline?","back":"Existing sunscreen effectiveness falls from 100% to 45% to 20% over about 4 hours."},{"id":"x-justify","topicId":"qvp","front":"Rule for every figure in a QVP?","back":"Have references or justification for it (they are hypotheses to validate)."},{"id":"x-sportsapp","topicId":"qvp","front":"Sports app pains and VP?","back":"Pains: students struggle to join clubs, clubs struggle to recruit. VP: students stay active and social, clubs regain members, revenue and morale."},{"id":"x-ctp","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Step 3 slide stresses profile gives?","back":"Categories and ranges, not a person."},{"id":"x-ride","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Ride-sharing profile example (key facts)?","back":"17-40, East of Ireland, medium/high social level, owns smartphone, early adopters, active on social networks."},{"id":"x-cake","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Urban Cake Shop three profiles and ages?","back":"Morning commuters 25-45, seasonal celebrators 35-55, one-time weddings 26-42 (about 15% of revenue)."},{"id":"x-cakemk","topicId":"end-user-profile","front":"Cake shop: how to market to each group?","back":"Commuters: loyalty and coupons; celebrators: email and Instagram; weddings: Facebook ads and newsletters."},{"id":"x-pers","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Persona slide: fields list?","back":"Picture, background, age, gender, behaviours, personality, goals, needs, motivations, pain points, spending habits."},{"id":"x-persbuy","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Persona fields beyond demographics (Semrush)?","back":"Bio, quote, pains, gains, factors influencing buying decisions, channels, jobs to be done, industry knowledge."},{"id":"x-charl","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Charlotte persona facts?","back":"23, law student, London, Logistician (ISTJ); goals save money, look presentable; scared of cyclists."},{"id":"x-purpose","topicId":"customer-persona","front":"Persona design maxim on slide?","back":"Design a product which has a purpose for the person using it."},{"id":"x-lcex","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","front":"Past-year use case examples named?","back":"Food Locker, SafeSip, The Crew, TFI Live, FlexFusion."},{"id":"x-bmcnum","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Lecture BMC fill-in numbering?","back":"1 segments, 2 value propositions, 3 channels, 4 customer relationships, 5 key activities, 6 key resources, 7 key partners, 8 revenue, 9 costs."},{"id":"x-getkeep","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Customer relationships in three words?","back":"Get, keep, grow customers.","hook":"GKG"},{"id":"x-cr","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Customer relationships Strategyzer question?","back":"Personal, automated, acquisitive or retentive relationship with each segment?"},{"id":"x-rsq","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Revenue streams question?","back":"What are customers really willing to pay for, how, and is it transactional or recurring?"},{"id":"x-ordr","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Ordr value proposition?","back":"Faster ordering, convenient payment, served in correct order, no long queues (pubs)."},{"id":"x-ordrrev","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"Ordr revenue streams?","back":"Percentage of each transaction, annual subscription, free installation/free app, advertising."},{"id":"x-linkedin","topicId":"business-model-canvas","front":"LinkedIn BMC revenue and relationship types?","back":"Revenue: premium subscriptions, hiring solutions, marketing solutions. Relationships: same-side and cross-side network effects."},{"id":"x-bottom","topicId":"business-model-basics","front":"Business model \"bottom line\" slide?","back":"No revenue model, no business; cure a pain, earn revenue; until someone pays, it is just an idea."},{"id":"x-seesaw","topicId":"business-model-basics","front":"Seesaw cartoon message?","back":"Balance time on value-creation innovation with value-capture innovation."},{"id":"x-align","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"Product alignment with persona table rows?","back":"Per persona priority: how to deliver new value, features, functions, benefits."},{"id":"x-brochex","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"Brochure examples shown in lecture?","back":"H.E.L.M.E.T smart cycling helmet, SLIM liquid inventory, Biegando local sports events app."},{"id":"x-mvtaj","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","front":"MVBP cartoon message?","back":"Skip the \"Taj Mahal\": build what gets customer feedback and cash."},{"id":"x-secret","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Step 10 nickname for the core?","back":"Secret sauce (castle and moat)."},{"id":"x-nirv","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Customer's nirvana metaphor?","back":"Your core builds the bridge to it; only you can cross."},{"id":"x-axis","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Which priority goes on which axis?","back":"Recipe text: priority 1 on x; printed template: priority 1 on y. Label clearly either way."},{"id":"x-sunspring","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"SunSpring chart axes and rivals?","back":"Efficiency (x) vs reliability (y, battery vs no battery); AquaSun, Spectra, WorldWater, Blue Spring."},{"id":"x-swotdef","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"SWOT: internal vs external?","back":"Internal: strengths, weaknesses. External: opportunities, threats."},{"id":"x-swotry","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Ryanair SWOT key points?","back":"S: point-to-point, young fleet, low cost, marketing. W: poor service/image. O: expansion, web. T: EU laws, fuel prices, competition."},{"id":"x-swotairb","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Airbnb SWOT key points?","back":"S: experience, affordable, unique. W: dissatisfaction, lawsuits, review dependency. O: urbanisation. T: competition, pandemic."},{"id":"x-swotamz","topicId":"core-competitive-position","front":"Amazon SWOT key points?","back":"S: service, IT, logistics, brand. W: brand confusion, free-shipping losses. O: China (Joyo), JVs. T: Kindle vs iPad, FX."},{"id":"x-trig","topicId":"windows-of-opportunity","front":"Five trigger pictures on slide?","back":"Expiring discount, time-limited offer, scarcity, free stuff, sales-rep interaction."},{"id":"x-drucker","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Drucker's 7 sources of innovation?","back":"Unexpected, incongruities, process needs, industry/market structure, demographics, perception/meaning/mood, new knowledge.","hook":"U-I-P-I-D-P-N"},{"id":"x-start","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Three ways to start a venture (cartoon)?","back":"Technological breakthrough, an idea, a passion."},{"id":"x-scart","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Science, craft or art?","back":"Craft: between science and art, so it can be taught."},{"id":"x-fish","topicId":"intro-innovation","front":"Course primary goal (proverb)?","back":"Not to give a fish but to teach how to fish."},{"id":"x-types7","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Seven circles in the innovation types diagram?","back":"Product, process, incremental, sustaining, frugal, reinnovation, disruptive."},{"id":"x-incr4","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Four types of incremental innovation?","back":"Product, process, service, business model."},{"id":"x-procout","topicId":"types-of-innovation","front":"Process innovation outcomes (diagram)?","back":"Efficiency and cost, quality, speed and time, flexibility, innovation enablement, competitive advantage."},{"id":"x-gi","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Good business idea groupings?","back":"Market driven, Feasible, Unique, Fundable, Innovative.","hook":"M-F-U-F-I"},{"id":"x-gi2","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Four kinds of \"innovative\" idea?","back":"Invention, Extension, Duplication, Synthesis.","hook":"I-E-D-S"},{"id":"x-gi3","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Feasible: six attributes?","back":"Attractive, achievable, durable, value creating, safe, affordable."},{"id":"x-gi4","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"Fundable: attributes?","back":"Revenue stream, sustainable, scalable or replicable, barriers to entry, growth potential, product pipeline, exit plan."},{"id":"x-right","topicId":"creativity-ideation","front":"General idea process letters?","back":"Research, Insight, Generate ideas, Hone, Test.","hook":"RIGHT"},{"id":"x-sitsub","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SIT subtraction definition?","back":"Elimination of an essential component of a product."},{"id":"x-substeps","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Subtraction steps?","back":"List internal components, remove one, ask should we/can we, modify the concept."},{"id":"x-sitdiv","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SIT division definition and types?","back":"Separate the product or components and rearrange them; functionally, physically, preservationally."},{"id":"x-sitpres","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Preservational division example?","back":"Cake divided into cupcakes: each smaller unit preserves the characteristics of the whole."},{"id":"x-sitmul","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SIT multiplication definition?","back":"Copy an element already in the product but change it in a counterintuitive way (P&G Febreze Plug example)."},{"id":"x-sitmul2","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Multiplication steps?","back":"List components, copy one and change it, look for potential benefits."},{"id":"x-situni","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Task unification definition and 3 types?","back":"Assign extra tasks to an existing resource: internal takes internal job, internal takes external job, external takes internal job."},{"id":"x-uni3","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Unification type 3 example?","back":"External takes internal job: billboard becomes the point of sale."},{"id":"x-uni2","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Unification type 2 example?","back":"Internal takes external job: car tyre alert (Nissan easy-fill)."},{"id":"x-sitad","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Attribute dependency definition?","back":"One thing changes as another thing changes."},{"id":"x-sitext","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"External vs internal attributes?","back":"External: environment/people (time, age, gender, preferences). Internal: product (size, shape, colour)."},{"id":"x-alarm","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"Alarm clock attribute-dependency example?","back":"As elapsed ringing time increases, loudness increases."},{"id":"x-ffp","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SIT Function Follows Form 3 steps?","back":"Start with existing situation, apply a thinking tool, ask \"should we do it and can we do it?\"."},{"id":"x-restau","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SCAMPER fast-food example?","back":"Adapt: sell real estate; Eliminate: self-service; Reverse: pay before eating."},{"id":"x-redesign","topicId":"ideation-techniques","front":"SCAMPER redesign exercise products?","back":"Desk lamp, toaster, washing machine, key fob, home camera, sleep mask, extension lead, safety shoe."},{"id":"x-snake","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Steps in theme \"How do you scale?\"","back":"14 follow-on TAM and 24 product plan."},{"id":"x-paying","topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","front":"Most important component of a new enterprise (slide)?","back":"Paying customers."},{"id":"x-jud","topicId":"pitch-video-deliverables","front":"What judges look for (Enterprise Ireland)?","back":"Market, USP, proof of concept, tested with real users, understanding of key financials."},{"id":"x-vid5","topicId":"pitch-video-deliverables","front":"Five video requirements?","back":"Define proposition, problem and how it works, innovation, target customer and why they buy, export potential."},{"id":"x-30","topicId":"pitch-video-deliverables","front":"Pitch: when to define the proposition?","back":"In the first 30 seconds of the 2-minute video."},{"id":"x-teamawd","topicId":"pitch-video-deliverables","front":"Student Entrepreneur Awards max team size?","back":"Five students (course teams are 2-4)."}],"methods":[{"id":"pick-beachhead","topicId":"beachhead-market","title":"Pick a beachhead market (scoring recipe)","when":"Asked to choose and justify one target segment for a startup idea","mnemonic":"LIST 5-6, SCORE 7 criteria, KILL show-stoppers, RANK, JUSTIFY","steps":["1. List 5-6 distinct candidate segments (end user + task, not industries).","2. Draw a table: rows = 7 criteria (economics, access, value proposition, complete product, competition, strategic value, personal alignment), columns = candidates.","3. Rate each cell Very High / High / Medium / Low / Show Stopper (or 5 to 1); give a 3-word reason where marks matter.","4. Eliminate any candidate with a show stopper.","5. Total (or weigh) the scores and rank 1 = most attractive.","6. Name the deciding factors and state the winner.","7. Name the runner-up as the first adjacent market and check the three market conditions for the winner (same product, same sales process, word of mouth)."],"example":{"problem":"Idea: DampDetect, a sensor that warns of hidden damp. Candidates: student landlords, social housing providers, private homeowners, hotels. Choose a beachhead.","solution":["Scores 1-5 in order (economics, access, value, complete product, competition, strategic value, personal alignment):","Student landlords: 4,4,5,4,3,4,5 = 29","Social housing providers: 5,2,5,3,3,5,3 = 26","Private homeowners: 2,2,3,5,2,2,3 = 19","Hotels: 3,3,2,3,2,3,2 = 18","Rank: 1 student landlords (29), 2 social housing (26), 3 homeowners (19), 4 hotels (18).","Winner: student landlords. Deciding factors: strong urgent pain (damp complaints and deposit disputes), reachable through landlord associations and college accommodation offices, team knows the student rental market, word of mouth among landlords.","Social housing is the best next market (big budgets but slow procurement: low access now)."]}},{"id":"segment-matrix","topicId":"market-segmentation","title":"Segment a market (matrix recipe)","when":"Asked to segment the market for an idea","mnemonic":"WHO does WHAT, how URGENT, how MANY, vs WHOM","steps":["1. State the idea and the core technology/benefit in one line.","2. Brainstorm segments by end user and task (different users, industries, use contexts).","3. For each of 4-6 segments fill: end user, task, benefit, urgency, example users, willingness to change, size (order of magnitude), competition incl. do-nothing.","4. Add complete-product needs and partners.","5. Cut to top 5-6 and pass to the beachhead scoring."],"example":{"problem":"Segment the market for a mobile app that tracks pregnancy for first-time mothers (Ireland).","solution":["Segments: first-time mothers 25-35 (urban), first-time mothers under 20, first-time mothers over 35 (high-risk), expectant partners, midwives/clinics, private maternity hospitals.","Row for first-time mothers 25-35: end user = the mother; task = track symptoms, appointments and milestones; benefit = reassurance and fewer worried calls; urgency = high for 9 months; size = about 10Ks (30,306 first-time mothers per year, subset 25-35); competition = existing apps, forums, midwife; do nothing = paper booklet.","Narrow to 5-6, then score on the beachhead criteria."]}},{"id":"calc-tam","topicId":"tam-sam-som","title":"Calculate TAM / SAM / SOM","when":"Asked to size the market or decide if the beachhead is big enough","mnemonic":"N x Q x F x P, then cut down for SAM and SOM","steps":["1. Define the beachhead end users precisely and count them (N) from data; cite the source.","2. Units bought at a time (Q) and how often per year (F). If one-time, divide by product life.","3. Fix a price (P); label it an assumption and cross-check with budget or comparables.","4. TAM = N x Q x F x P (EUR per year).","5. SAM = TAM x fraction you can serve (geography, channel, price).","6. SOM = SAM x realistic share (capacity, competition) in year 1-3.","7. Interpret: is it big enough to be interesting? Cross-check top-down."],"example":{"problem":"DampDetect: 18,000 student landlords in Ireland (assume), each with 3 rental properties, sensor price EUR 60 per property, life 4 years. Serviceable: Galway, Cork, Dublin = 55% of landlords. Realistic share in year 3: 8%. Compute TAM, SAM, SOM.","solution":["Units: N x Q = 18,000 x 3 = 54,000 sensors in use.","Annualised price: 60 / 4 = EUR 15 per sensor per year (F = 1).","TAM = 54,000 x 15 = EUR 810,000 per year (one-time equivalent EUR 3,240,000).","SAM = 810,000 x 0.55 = EUR 445,500 per year.","SOM = 445,500 x 0.08 = EUR 35,640 per year.","Interpretation: a small beachhead, fine for a first segment but needs add-on subscription revenue or adjacent markets (social housing) to grow."]}},{"id":"tam-per-user","topicId":"tam-sam-som","title":"Annual revenue per user (workbook method)","when":"Asked to estimate revenue per end user using the three data points","mnemonic":"ONE-TIME, BUDGET, COMPARABLE, then consensus","steps":["1. One-time charge: (price x units per user) / life in years.","2. Budget: user budget x % that reasonably goes to this solution.","3. Comparables: list comparable products and convert to annual revenue.","4. Compare the data points; choose a consensus range and explain how you got it.","5. TAM = number of end users x chosen annual revenue."],"example":{"problem":"Gym-chain smart rack: price 1,200, 1 unit, life 4 years; budget 10,000 with 5% available; comparable spend 400 per year. 3,000 end users.","solution":["(1) 1,200 x 1 / 4 = 300 per year.","(2) 10,000 x 0.05 = 500 per year.","(3) comparable = 400 per year.","Consensus range 300-500; take 400.","TAM = 3,000 x 400 = 1,200,000 per year."]}},{"id":"write-persona","topicId":"customer-persona","title":"Write a persona","when":"Asked to create the persona or end-user profile for a beachhead","mnemonic":"NAME, STORY, GOALS, PAINS, PRIORITIES, REACH","steps":["1. Base it on the End-User Profile and research from the beachhead (interviews, observation).","2. Give one fictional named person and a photo description.","3. Demographics: age, role, location, income, family.","4. Psychographics and behaviours: values, habits, tech attitude, spending habits, proxy products.","5. Goals and how she measures success (gains).","6. Pains: bad day, fears, obstacles, responsibilities.","7. Weighted priorities (sum 100) and where to reach her (watering holes)."],"example":{"problem":"Persona for student landlords (DampDetect).","solution":["Name: Maeve Ryan, 47, accountant and landlord of 3 student houses in Galway (income 85k, married, 2 teenagers).","Psychographics: cautious, protects her pension asset, dislikes call-outs, uses WhatsApp and the Daft.ie portal, owns a smart thermostat (proxy product).","Goals: full occupancy, no deposit disputes, less time on repairs.","Pains: tenants report damp late, repair bills EUR 4,000+, fears property-damage claims, is 2 hours from the houses.","Priorities: avoid repair cost 40, save time 25, keep tenants happy 20, minimise upfront cost 15 = 100.","Reach: landlord association events, Facebook groups, letting agents."]}},{"id":"write-qvp","topicId":"qvp","title":"Write a Quantified Value Proposition","when":"Asked for the QVP or to quantify the benefit","mnemonic":"PRIORITY #1, AS-IS X, TO-BE Y, DIFFERENCE, PROOF","steps":["1. Take the persona's #1 priority and its metric.","2. Measure/estimate the As-Is state X (current solution or do nothing).","3. Estimate the To-Be state Y with your product.","4. Compute the difference and the percentage; add the money or time per period.","5. State the evidence that supports it (pilot, benchmark, research) and label estimates as hypotheses.","6. Check that no other key priority gets worse.","7. Write it as one sentence."],"example":{"problem":"Maeve's top priority: avoid repair cost from late-detected damp. As-Is: damp found after 6 weeks on average, repair cost EUR 4,200. To-Be: detected within 3 days, repair EUR 600.","solution":["Time to detection: 42 days to 3 days = 39 days earlier (93% faster).","Repair cost: 4,200 - 600 = EUR 3,600 saved per incident (3,600 / 4,200 = 85.7% lower).","If 1 in 6 properties has an incident each year, a 3-property landlord saves 0.5 x 3,600 = EUR 1,800 per year, against a sensor cost of EUR 15 per sensor per year.","QVP: \"For landlords like Maeve, DampDetect cuts damp repair cost from EUR 4,200 to EUR 600 per incident by detecting it in 3 days instead of 6 weeks.\" Support: pilot on 20 homes (to be validated). Other priorities: install time 5 minutes, no loss of tenant satisfaction."]}},{"id":"fill-bmc","topicId":"business-model-canvas","title":"Fill in the Business Model Canvas","when":"Asked to complete or critique a BMC for a startup","mnemonic":"SEGMENT, VALUE, CHANNEL, RELATIONSHIP, REVENUE, RESOURCE, ACTIVITY, PARTNER, COST","steps":["1. Customer segments: beachhead end user and economic buyer (one entry per side if two-sided).","2. Value propositions: the QVP per segment.","3. Channels and customer relationships: how found, sold, delivered, retained.","4. Revenue streams: who pays, how (unit, subscription, fee), indicative price.","5. Key resources, key activities, key partnerships needed to deliver the value.","6. Cost structure: biggest cost drivers, fixed vs variable.","7. Consistency check: every value proposition has a segment, channel and revenue stream; every cost maps to an activity or resource."],"example":{"problem":"Complete a BMC for DampDetect.","solution":["Customer segments: student landlords (buyer = user), later social housing providers.","Value proposition: detect damp in 3 days not 6 weeks; save about EUR 3,600 per incident.","Channels: landlord association events, letting agents, online shop, Daft/Facebook ads.","Customer relationships: self-service app, alerts, annual check call, upsell extra rooms.","Revenue: EUR 60 sensor sale plus EUR 2 per month alert subscription.","Key resources: sensor design IP, cloud alert platform, engineers, working capital for stock.","Key activities: hardware design, app development, customer acquisition, support.","Key partnerships: sensor manufacturer, letting agents, landlord association.","Cost structure: cost of goods about EUR 22 per sensor (variable), salaries and cloud (fixed), marketing."]}},{"id":"lifecycle-recipe","topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","title":"Write the full life-cycle use case","when":"Asked to describe the customer journey for the persona","mnemonic":"10 stages x (who, when, where, how)","steps":["1. Use the persona by name.","2. List the 10 stages: need, find, analyse, acquire, pay, install, use, value, buy more, tell others.","3. For each give who, when, where, how in a short line.","4. Flag the barrier to adoption at each stage.","5. Note what each stage tells you (pricing, go-to-market, COCA, CLV)."],"example":{"problem":"Life cycle for Maeve and DampDetect.","solution":["1 Need: tenant complains of smell in winter; trigger = deposit dispute last year.","2 Find: searches Google and asks the landlord association Facebook group.","3 Analyse: compares with a handheld meter and a surveyor visit; wants proof of savings.","4-5 Acquire/pay: online order, card payment, shipped in 3 days.","6 Install: tenant fixes it to the wall in 5 minutes using app instructions.","7 Use: app alerts Maeve and tenant when humidity stays above threshold.","8 Value: sees an early alert avoid a EUR 4,200 repair; annual summary.","9 Buy more: adds sensors to her other two houses.","10 Tell others: posts in the Facebook group, refers friends for a discount.","Barriers: trust in claims (stage 3), tenant cooperation (stage 6)."]}},{"id":"position-chart","topicId":"core-competitive-position","title":"Draw a competitive position chart","when":"Asked to position the product against alternatives","mnemonic":"TOP 2 PRIORITIES, GOOD vs BAD, PLOT incl. DO-NOTHING","steps":["1. Take persona priorities 1 and 2 as x and y axes.","2. Label low (bad) near the origin and high (good) far from it.","3. List direct competitors, indirect alternatives, future entrants and do nothing.","4. Place each with a one-line justification.","5. Place your product top-right and state what stops competitors copying (your core).","6. Add the supporting SWOT if asked."],"example":{"problem":"Position DampDetect for Maeve: priority 1 = early detection, priority 2 = ease of use.","solution":["Do nothing: slow detection (about 6 weeks), very easy. Low x, high y.","Handheld damp meter: manual checks, so slow detection and hard to use consistently. Low x, low y.","Surveyor visit: accurate but occasional, so medium detection, low ease. Medium x, low y.","Competing smart sensor: fast detection, complex setup. High x, low-medium y.","DampDetect: 3-day detection with 5-minute install. High x, high y.","Core: humidity-pattern algorithm trained on proprietary data (unique, grows, important)."]}},{"id":"product-brochure","topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","title":"Write the high-level product spec (brochure)","when":"Asked to outline the product spec or brochure for the beachhead","mnemonic":"NAME, HEADLINE BENEFIT, 2 MORE, MAGNITUDE, ACT (no price!)","steps":["1. Name and tagline.","2. A visual showing how it works in the customer's workflow.","3. Headline benefit tied to the persona's #1 priority.","4. Two additional benefits.","5. Magnitude: pilot targets from the QVP.","6. Call to action (where to buy or find out more).","7. Check: benefits not features, no price, nothing built yet."],"example":{"problem":"Brochure outline for DampDetect.","solution":["Name: DampDetect, \"Catch damp in days, not weeks\".","Visual: sensor on a bedroom wall, phone alert, before/after repair.","Headline: Cut damp repair bills by up to 85% (pilot target).","Additional: 5-minute install; automatic evidence report for deposit disputes.","Magnitude: detection in 3 days vs 6 weeks; to be validated with 20 pilot homes.","Call to action: Join the landlord pilot at the association meeting."]}},{"id":"essay-structure","topicId":"intro-innovation","title":"Answer structure: define-explain-example essays","when":"Asked to discuss, compare or explain a concept (innovation types, creativity, entrepreneurship teachable?)","mnemonic":"DEFINE, MECHANISM, EXAMPLE, EVALUATE","steps":["1. Define each term in one sentence in lecture vocabulary.","2. Explain the mechanism or the steps (numbered).","3. Give one named example per item (3M, Airbnb, GE ECG...).","4. For compare questions use a table: dimension, A, B; then one sentence on when each applies.","5. Evaluate: advantage, limitation, link to the startup being designed.","6. Finish with a one-line conclusion answering the question asked."],"example":{"problem":"Distinguish invention and innovation (6 marks).","solution":["Definition (2): invention = a creative idea; innovation = turning it into a feasible product or service that satisfies customers.","Equation (1): innovation = invention x commercialisation; zero commercialisation means zero innovation.","Example (2): Spencer Silver invented the low-tack adhesive at 3M in 1968; it became innovation only when sold as Post-it notes.","Conclusion (1): value to customers, not novelty alone, is what makes an innovation."]}},{"id":"apply-scamper","topicId":"ideation-techniques","title":"Apply SCAMPER to a product","when":"Asked to generate ideas for improving a product","mnemonic":"S-C-A-M-P-E-R, one idea per letter","steps":["1. Name the product and the user.","2. Substitute: a part, material or person to replace.","3. Combine: two features or products to merge.","4. Adapt: copy an idea from another field.","5. Modify (magnify/minify): size, shape, more or less of a feature.","6. Put to another use: other market or user.","7. Eliminate, then Reverse; finish by selecting with criteria."],"example":{"problem":"SCAMPER a reusable coffee cup.","solution":["Substitute: plastic lid with a bamboo-fibre lid.","Combine: cup with a built-in loyalty NFC tag.","Adapt: telescopic collapsing like a camping cup.","Modify: smaller 150 ml cup for espresso.","Put to other use: soup cup for office lunches.","Eliminate: remove the sleeve using double-wall design.","Reverse: cafe provides the cup deposit on the way out instead of upfront.","Select with criteria such as cost, safety, differentiation."]}},{"id":"apply-sit","topicId":"ideation-techniques","title":"Apply the five SIT tools to a product","when":"Asked to generate ideas with Systematic Inventive Thinking, or to name and illustrate SIT tools","mnemonic":"S-D-M-U-A: Subtract, Divide, Multiply, Unify, Attribute; then ask \"Should we? Can we?\"","steps":["1. Start with an existing situation: name the product and list its internal components (and external ones for unification).","2. Subtraction: remove one apparently essential component, then modify the concept so the remainder still works.","3. Division: split the product or a component (functionally, physically or preservationally) and rearrange the parts.","4. Multiplication: copy a component and change it in a counterintuitive way.","5. Task unification: give an existing component (or an external one) an extra job.","6. Attribute dependency: list external and internal attributes, then link two: \"as attribute A changes, so does attribute B\".","7. For each idea look for potential benefits and ask: should we do it, and can we do it? Keep the best and state why."],"example":{"problem":"Use SIT on a bicycle helmet.","solution":["Components: shell, foam liner, straps, buckle, vents, visor.","Subtraction: remove the straps and use a snug-fit band or magnetic closure; benefit: faster to put on for commuters.","Division: physically split the shell into a foldable segmented shell; benefit: fits in a bag (preservational: each segment still protects).","Multiplication: copy the rear vent as a rear light with a different role (signalling); benefit: visibility.","Task unification: the buckle takes the job of a crash sensor (internal takes internal job) or a phone app takes the job of a bike lock reminder (external takes internal job).","Attribute dependency: external attribute = ambient light; internal attribute = brightness of the rear light; as ambient light falls, brightness rises.","Selection: keep attribute dependency (cheap, safe, clear benefit) and the foldable shell for later; ask \"should we, can we?\" for each."]}},{"id":"swot-analysis","topicId":"core-competitive-position","title":"Write a SWOT analysis","when":"Asked to do a SWOT for a startup or a named company","mnemonic":"Inside = S and W; Outside = O and T; positive, negative, then act","steps":["1. Draw four boxes; label internal (Strengths, Weaknesses) and external (Opportunities, Threats).","2. Strengths: what you do well, what separates you from competitors, key resources, IP and assets.","3. Weaknesses: what you lack, what competitors do better, resource limits, unclear USP.","4. Opportunities: underserved markets, few competitors, emerging needs, positive trends and press.","5. Threats: emerging competitors, regulation, negative press, changing customer attitudes, costs.","6. Give 3-5 specific points per box tied to the scenario (not generic).","7. Finish with one line: how to use strengths on opportunities and how to defend against threats."],"example":{"problem":"SWOT for a smart weightlifting rack startup selling to high-end chain gyms.","solution":["Strengths: proprietary computer-vision form models; passive camera with no wearables; fits any rack in minutes.","Weaknesses: no contacts with chain head offices; small team and cash; hardware must be very high quality.","Opportunities: 3,000 high-end chains in the US (12k+ units TAM); 34 million lifters; follow-on markets in budget gyms.","Threats: large fitness-equipment makers copying; injury-liability claims; gyms preferring human trainers.","Action: use the model IP strength on the chain-gym opportunity via a pilot, and counter copying by growing the training-data moat."]}},{"id":"tam-three-levels","topicId":"tam-sam-som","title":"Size total, served and target market (rental units style)","when":"Asked to show total market, serviceable market and target market with revenue","mnemonic":"Everyone, Reachable, First buyers: units x people x price x 12","steps":["1. Define the unit (e.g. rental unit) and the price per user per period (e.g. USD 1 per month).","2. Total available market: all units in the country x users per unit x price x 12.","3. Served available market: only units your sales channel can reach (e.g. one city).","4. Target market: the first buyers (e.g. large high-rise buildings in that city); apply adoption %.","5. Convert each level to annual revenue and label assumptions and sources.","6. Sanity check: is the target level big enough for positive cash flow and small enough to dominate?"],"example":{"problem":"US rentals: 42.58M units (all US), 0.59M in Manhattan, 190K high-rise units over 100 per building. Assume 2 residents per unit, USD 1 per user per month, 30% adoption in the target market.","solution":["Total: 42,580,000 x 2 x 1 x 12 = USD 1,021,920,000, about USD 1.02B per year.","Served (Manhattan): 590,000 x 2 x 1 x 12 = USD 14,160,000, about USD 14.2M.","Target: 190,000 x 2 x 0.30 x 1 x 12 = USD 1,368,000, about USD 1.4M.","Reading: the target is a thin but winnable slice (about 0.13% of the total) good for a first beachhead; state sources and assumptions."]}},{"id":"qvp-time-saving","topicId":"qvp","title":"Quantify a QVP as a percentage improvement","when":"Given as-is and to-be figures and asked to express the value proposition","mnemonic":"(X minus Y) over X, in the same unit","steps":["1. Pick persona priority #1 and the metric (time, cost, loss, errors).","2. Put every stage in the same unit (days) before adding.","3. Compute as-is total X and to-be total Y.","4. Value = X - Y (absolute) and (X - Y) / X x 100 (percent).","5. Check other priorities do not worsen, and give a source for every figure.","6. Write the sentence: \"moves metric from X to Y, a Z% improvement\"."],"example":{"problem":"Product development: as-is ideation 3 weeks, design 10 days, production 1 week, commercialisation 1 month (30 days). To-be: 2 weeks, 5 days, 1 week, 1 month.","solution":["As-is in days: 21 + 10 + 7 + 30 = 68.","To-be in days: 14 + 5 + 7 + 30 = 56.","Saving: 68 - 56 = 12 days; 12 / 68 = 17.6%, quoted as about 18%.","Stage view: ideation 7/21 = 33%, design 5/10 = 50%.","Cattle example: 63k - 40k = 23k saving (about 37% lower disease loss) for USD 7 per cow."]}}],"practice":[],"mocks":[{"id":"mock1","title":"Mock Paper 1","duration":120,"instructions":"FORMAT ASSUMED: no real paper was supplied for CT4107. This mock assumes: 120 minutes, answer any 3 of 5 questions, 20 marks each. Show assumptions and arithmetic. Original questions.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":20,"scenario":"FarmFlow is an app that lets small farms share tractors, balers and sprayers. Owners list machines and book them by the day; a booking service handles insurance and payment.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"market-segmentation","text":"Segment the market for FarmFlow. Identify at least five segments and, for two of them, state the end user, task and benefit.","outline":["Five distinct segments by end user and task: dairy farmers, tillage farmers, sheep/hill farmers, farm contractors, machinery co-ops, agricultural colleges (1 each up to 4)","End user is a real person, not an organisation (1)","Two segments with end user, task, benefit (2)","Mentions urgency, size or competition including do nothing (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":10,"topicId":"beachhead-market","text":"Choose a beachhead market using the seven beachhead criteria. Present a scoring table for four candidates and justify your choice.","outline":["Seven criteria named: economics, access, value proposition, complete product, competition, strategic value, personal alignment (2)","Table with 4 candidates and consistent scores (3)","Show stoppers considered/eliminated (1)","Ranking and winner (1)","Justification with deciding factors tied to scenario (2)","Mentions the three market conditions or adjacent next market (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":4,"topicId":"beachhead-market","text":"Why should a startup select only one beachhead market?","outline":["Focus limited resources (1)","One tailored message and persona (1)","Word of mouth within one community and dominance (1)","Base to attack adjacent markets (1)"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":20,"scenario":"Use FarmFlow with the beachhead \"dairy farms in Munster with 60-150 cows\". Assume 6,000 such farms, 1 booking account per farm, an average of 12 bookings a year, and a platform fee of EUR 40 per booking. 70% of farms are in the counties you can serve first; you expect 10% of those in year 3.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":4,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Define TAM, SAM and SOM.","outline":["TAM: annual revenue at 100% share of the beachhead (1)","SAM: part you can serve given geography, price, distribution (1)","SOM: part of SAM you can realistically capture (1)","TAM >= SAM >= SOM (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Calculate TAM, SAM and SOM in EUR per year. Show formula and arithmetic.","outline":["Formula N x Q x F x P (2)","TAM = 6,000 x 1 x 12 x 40 = EUR 2,880,000 (2)","SAM = 2,880,000 x 0.7 = EUR 2,016,000 (2)","SOM = 2,016,000 x 0.1 = EUR 201,600 (2)"]},{"label":"c","marks":4,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Compare top-down and bottom-up TAM estimation. Which did you use?","outline":["Top-down: start from industry total and cut by percentages (1)","Bottom-up: build from units, frequency and price (1)","Bottom-up more credible, ties to real buyers; top-down good for sanity check (1)","States bottom-up used (1)"]},{"label":"d","marks":4,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Is the beachhead market large enough to be interesting? Comment on your result and how you would grow it.","outline":["Interprets SOM about EUR 200k as small for a company (1)","Notes assumptions need validation (1)","Growth: more bookings, subscription, adjacent segments such as tillage or contractors (2)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":20,"scenario":"Continue with FarmFlow and dairy farmers.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"customer-persona","text":"Write a persona for the beachhead. Include demographics, psychographics, goals, pains and priorities.","outline":["Named fictional individual with background (1)","Demographics (1)","Psychographics and behaviours (1)","Goals/gains (1)","Pains (1)","Weighted priorities summing to 100 (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":4,"topicId":"end-user-profile","text":"Distinguish an end-user profile from a persona and state the six parts of the profile.","outline":["Profile = narrow group average; persona = one representative fictional person (2)","Demographics, psychographics, proxy products, watering holes, day in the life, priorities (2)"]},{"label":"c","marks":10,"topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","text":"Describe the full life-cycle use case for your persona. Give all ten stages with who, when, where and how for at least five of them.","outline":["Ten stages named: need, find, analyse, acquire, pay, install, use, value, buy more, tell others (3)","Five stages with who/when/where/how in scenario terms (5)","Identifies adoption barriers (trust, insurance, timing in season) (1)","Links stages to later steps (pricing, COCA, go-to-market) (1)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":20,"scenario":"Continue with FarmFlow.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"qvp","text":"Write a quantified value proposition for FarmFlow. State assumptions about the as-is and to-be states.","outline":["Persona top priority identified, e.g. cost of machinery access (2)","As-Is state with number, e.g. owning a baler costs EUR 9,000 per year for 8 days use (2)","To-Be state with number (2)","Difference computed and one-sentence QVP (1)","Evidence/validation and other priorities not worsened (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"core-competitive-position","text":"Define your core and explain whether it forms a competitive moat. Draw (describe) a competitive position chart with two priorities.","outline":["Core: what you do better and protect (1)","Candidate core e.g. network of machine owners and trust/insurance data (1)","Moat tests: unique, grows, important (1)","Axes are persona priorities (1)","Competitors and do nothing plotted (1)","Own product top-right with reason (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","text":"Outline the high-level product specification and define the minimum viable business product.","outline":["Spec: visual, benefits not features, customer view, no price, do not build yet (2)","Brochure items: headline benefit, two more, magnitude, call to action (2)","MVBP: customer gets value, pays, starts feedback loop (2)"]}]},{"n":5,"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"business-model-canvas","text":"Complete a Business Model Canvas for FarmFlow, naming all nine blocks with one or two entries each.","outline":["All nine blocks present (4.5)","Two-sided segments: machine owners and renters (1)","Revenue: fee per booking / subscription (1)","Resources: platform, insurance partner, trust data (1)","Costs: platform dev, insurance, acquisition, fixed vs variable (1)","Internal consistency (1.5)"]},{"label":"b","marks":4,"topicId":"business-model-basics","text":"Distinguish a business model from a business plan.","outline":["Model: how value is created, delivered, captured (1)","Plan: detailed strategy and multi-year financials (1)","Model first gives earlier customer feedback (1)","Plan is easier to write once the model is clear (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"types-of-innovation","text":"Is FarmFlow a product, process or business model innovation? Compare incremental and disruptive innovation with an example of each.","outline":["FarmFlow mainly business model / sharing platform (1)","Incremental: small improvements for existing customers, e.g. phone camera upgrades (2)","Disruptive: low-end entrant overtakes incumbents, e.g. Airbnb (2)","Classification justified (1)"]}]}]},{"id":"mock2","title":"Mock Paper 2","duration":120,"instructions":"FORMAT ASSUMED: no real paper was supplied for CT4107. This mock assumes: 120 minutes, answer any 3 of 5 questions, 20 marks each. Show assumptions and arithmetic. Original questions.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":20,"scenario":"BinBuddy is a sensor fitted to commercial wheelie bins that reports fill level so waste companies collect only when needed. Possible customers: restaurants, hotels, supermarkets, university campuses, apartment management companies, and local councils.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"market-segmentation","text":"Complete a segmentation matrix row (end user, task, benefit, urgency, size, competition) for two of the segments.","outline":["End user is a person, e.g. facilities manager (1 each)","Task and benefit (1 each)","Urgency, size order of magnitude, competition incl. do nothing (1 each segment)"]},{"label":"b","marks":10,"topicId":"beachhead-market","text":"Score four segments against the seven beachhead criteria in a table, rank them and recommend a beachhead.","outline":["Seven criteria (2)","Four candidates and consistent ratings using the Very High to Show Stopper scale (3)","Show stopper or elimination (1)","Ranking (1)","Winner with deciding factors (2)","Next adjacent market (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":4,"topicId":"beachhead-market","text":"Explain the three conditions that define a market and test your chosen beachhead against them.","outline":["Buys the same product (1)","Similar sales process (1)","Word of mouth exists (1)","Applied to the chosen segment (1)"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":20,"scenario":"Beachhead: apartment management companies in Ireland. Assume 1,500 companies, each manages an average of 40 bins; sensor price EUR 55 per bin, expected life 5 years, plus EUR 1 per bin per month subscription. A competitor survey suggests customers budget EUR 20 per bin per year for waste optimisation, of which 40% could go to this solution.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Annualise the revenue per bin using (i) the one-time charge, (ii) the subscription, and (iii) the budget method.","outline":["(i) 55/5 = EUR 11 per bin per year (2)","(ii) 1 x 12 = EUR 12 per year, so one-time plus subscription = EUR 23 (2)","(iii) 20 x 0.4 = EUR 8 per year (2)"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Choose a consensus revenue per bin with reasons and compute TAM.","outline":["Notes range 8-23 (1)","Chooses a defensible figure, e.g. EUR 15 and explains that budget caps willingness to pay (2)","Units = 1,500 x 40 = 60,000 bins (1)","TAM = 60,000 x 15 = EUR 900,000 per year (2)"]},{"label":"c","marks":4,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"If only companies in Dublin and Cork (45% of the market) can be served and you expect a 12% share, compute SAM and SOM.","outline":["SAM = 900,000 x 0.45 = EUR 405,000 (2)","SOM = 405,000 x 0.12 = EUR 48,600 (2)"]},{"label":"d","marks":4,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Discuss two weaknesses of this estimate.","outline":["Assumed price and share not validated (1)","Bins per company average hides variation (1)","Ignores competitors or replacement cycle (1)","Suggest validation by interviews / landing page (1)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"customer-persona","text":"Create a persona for an apartment property manager, and list the six parts of an end-user profile.","outline":["Named persona with background and demographics (2)","Psychographics, goals, pains (3)","Weighted priorities summing to 100 (1)","Six profile parts named (2)"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"primary-market-research","text":"Describe three primary market research methods you would use to validate the persona and segment.","outline":["Three methods, e.g. contextual interviews at the bin store, observation of collection days, landing page test (1 each)","What each reveals (0.5 each)","PMR never ends and underlies the steps (0.5)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","text":"Give stages 1 to 5 of the full life-cycle use case for the persona.","outline":["Need and catalyst, e.g. overflowing bins complaint (1)","Find (1)","Analyse competitors/alternatives (1)","Acquire (1)","Pay (1)","Who/when/where/how specified (1)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"qvp","text":"Write a QVP for BinBuddy. As-is: 3 fixed collections per week cost EUR 30 each; to-be: collect when 80% full. Assume 2 collections per week on average with the sensor.","outline":["Priority identified: collection cost (1)","As-is annual cost 3 x 30 x 52 = EUR 4,680 per bin cluster (2)","To-be 2 x 30 x 52 = EUR 3,120 (2)","Saving EUR 1,560 per year = 33% (2)","One-sentence QVP and note on validation (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"core-competitive-position","text":"Define a competitive moat with its three dimensions and suggest a core for BinBuddy. Include the competitive position chart axes.","outline":["Moat definition (1)","Unique, grows, important (2)","Core suggestion e.g. route optimisation data (1)","Axes from persona priorities, do nothing plotted (2)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"windows-of-opportunity","text":"Identify a window of opportunity and a trigger for selling BinBuddy.","outline":["Define window and trigger (2)","Window e.g. waste-charge increase, contract renewal (2)","Trigger e.g. free pilot month, limited places (2)"]}]},{"n":5,"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"creativity-ideation","text":"Describe the five-step creative process and the four elements of creativity.","outline":["Objective, data gathering, problem design, ideation, selection (2.5)","One-line each description (2.5)","Problem finding/solving, relevance, value, novelty (2)","Spend time on problem first (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"ideation-techniques","text":"Apply four SCAMPER prompts to a wheelie bin.","outline":["Four valid prompts with distinct, sensible ideas (1.5 each)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"intro-innovation","text":"Explain Innovation = Invention x Commercialisation and illustrate with an example.","outline":["Invention is a creative idea (1)","Innovation makes it feasible and valued by customers (1)","Product of both: zero if not commercialised (2)","Example e.g. 3M adhesive to Post-it (2)"]}]}]},{"id":"mock3","title":"Mock Paper 3","duration":120,"instructions":"FORMAT ASSUMED: no real paper was supplied for CT4107. This mock assumes: 120 minutes, answer any 3 of 5 questions, 20 marks each. Show assumptions and arithmetic. Original questions.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":20,"scenario":"TutorMatch is an app that matches secondary-school Leaving Cert students with university students as paid tutors, with scheduling, payment and ratings built in. Candidate segments: Leaving Cert students in Dublin, rural Leaving Cert students, parents of Junior Cert students, adult learners, international students, and schools.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":4,"topicId":"market-segmentation","text":"What is the difference between the end user and the economic buyer? Identify both for two of the segments.","outline":["End user uses product; economic buyer pays (2)","Two segments with both identified, e.g. student/parent (2)"]},{"label":"b","marks":10,"topicId":"beachhead-market","text":"Select a beachhead market. Show a scoring table of at least four segments against the seven criteria and discuss the deciding factors.","outline":["Seven criteria (2)","Table 4 candidates with ratings (3)","Show stopper treatment (1)","Ranking (1)","Deciding factors tied to access, value, competition, strategic value (3)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"primary-market-research","text":"Describe how you would validate your choice with primary market research before committing.","outline":["Customer interviews with open questions (2)","Landing page test with email/payment capture (2)","Observation or user test (1)","Never-ending iterative research (1)"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":20,"scenario":"Beachhead: Leaving Cert students in Dublin who want maths tuition. Assume 25,000 Leaving Cert students in Dublin, 30% take paid tuition, those who do buy 20 sessions a year, and TutorMatch takes EUR 6 commission per session. You can reach 60% of them through partner schools and expect 5% share in year 3.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Compute TAM in EUR per year with a clear bottom-up calculation.","outline":["Users N = 25,000 x 0.30 = 7,500 (2)","F = 20 sessions, price EUR 6 (2)","TAM = 7,500 x 20 x 6 = EUR 900,000 (4)"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Compute SAM and SOM.","outline":["SAM = 900,000 x 0.6 = EUR 540,000 (3)","SOM = 540,000 x 0.05 = EUR 27,000 (3)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"tam-sam-som","text":"Explain what the result says about the viability of the beachhead and propose a follow-on market.","outline":["SOM small, so revenue limited (2)","Assumptions to validate (1)","Follow-on: other counties, other subjects, Junior Cert (2)","Beachhead small is fine if it yields references and word of mouth (1)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"customer-persona","text":"Write a persona for the beachhead and explain why a persona is used.","outline":["Named persona with demographics, psychographics (2)","Goals and pains (2)","Priorities weighted (1)","Why: focus, touchstone for decisions (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"life-cycle-use-case","text":"Describe stages 6 to 10 of the full life-cycle use case, each with who, when, where, how.","outline":["Install/set up (1.5)","Use and get value (1.5)","Determine value gained (1.5)","Buy more (1.5)","Tell others (1.5)","Who/when/where/how specified (0.5)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"windows-of-opportunity","text":"Explain windows of opportunity and triggers and give one of each for TutorMatch.","outline":["Window definition (1)","Trigger definition (1)","Window e.g. start of Leaving Cert year / mock exams (2)","Trigger e.g. first session free, limited tutors (2)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"qvp","text":"Write a QVP for TutorMatch using a priority of your choice and assumed numbers. Explain why quantification matters.","outline":["Priority #1 e.g. time to find a qualified tutor or cost per hour (1)","As-is X with number (2)","To-be Y with number (2)","Difference and sentence (1)","Why: cannot demonstrate value without measuring (1)","Validate with customers / no priority decreases (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":6,"topicId":"product-spec-mvbp","text":"Describe what a high-level product specification should contain and what it should avoid. Define the MVBP.","outline":["Visual, customer view, benefits (2)","Avoid price, detail, building (1)","MVBP: value, payment, feedback loop (3)"]},{"label":"c","marks":6,"topicId":"core-competitive-position","text":"State TutorMatch's core and plot its competitive position against three alternatives and do nothing.","outline":["Core identified and moat test (2)","Axes: persona priorities (1)","Three alternatives (e.g. grinds agencies, YouTube, school study) plus do nothing (2)","Own position justified (1)"]}]},{"n":5,"marks":20,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"business-model-canvas","text":"Fill in a Business Model Canvas for TutorMatch. Explain how the revenue streams and cost structure link to the other blocks.","outline":["All nine blocks (5)","Two-sided: students/parents and tutors (1)","Revenue: commission, premium subscription (1)","Costs: platform, payment fees, acquisition (1)","Links: costs map to activities/resources; revenue to segments (2)"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"disciplined-entrepreneurship","text":"Outline the Disciplined Entrepreneurship process and argue that entrepreneurship can be taught.","outline":["24 steps and six questions (2)","Steps 1-11 order (1)","Process not magic (Aulet) (1)","Common language and structure (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":5,"topicId":"types-of-innovation","text":"Distinguish frugal, reinnovation and disruptive innovation with examples.","outline":["Frugal: low cost for overlooked consumers, GE ECG (1.5)","Reinnovation: reuse materials, ocean plastic yarn (1.5)","Disruptive: low-end entrant displaces incumbent, Airbnb (1.5)","Distinction clear (0.5)"]}]}]}]};
